1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,436
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,436 --> 00:00:04,372
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,372 --> 00:00:07,760
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,160
PHILLIP ISOLA: We're going to continue on today and this week

5
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,000
with generative models.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,200
So today will be on variational autoencoders.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,840
And we're going to give a perspective

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:27,440
that generative modeling and representation learning

9
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:29,320
are very tightly coupled.

10
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,833
If you can solve the generative modeling problem,

11
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:34,000
it should help you solve the representation learning

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,337
problem and vice versa.

13
00:00:35,337 --> 00:00:36,920
And variational autoencoders are going

14
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:38,587
to be one model that really puts the two

15
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:42,880
ideas together and solves them both at the same time.

16
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,120
And this lecture is a little bit different

17
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,080
than some of the last few, in that

18
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:49,440
rather than covering a lot of different topics

19
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,500
in some superficial level of detail,

20
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,180
we're going to just go deep on VAEs,

21
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,480
on variational autoencoders.

22
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,480
And we'll also talk a little bit about the types

23
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,840
of representations that are learned

24
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,715
by generative models at the end and this idea

25
00:01:00,715 --> 00:01:01,740
of disentanglement.

26
00:01:04,319 --> 00:01:07,005
So this is a picture I've shown a few different times

27
00:01:07,005 --> 00:01:08,880
throughout all of the representation learning

28
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,680
and generative modeling lectures in this class.

29
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,840
It's how I like to think about the two problems, where

30
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,400
representation learning is going from raw data

31
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,120
to some nicer, structured, abstracted representation.

32
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:25,040
And generative modeling is going from a nice, simple, abstracted

33
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,160
representation to data.

34
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:29,960
And I've said that these two things can

35
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:33,480
be kind of thought of as inverses to some degree, not

36
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:36,920
necessarily inverses in the sense of a mathematical function

37
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:40,600
inverse because the direction from data to embeddings

38
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,840
is many to one, and the direction from embeddings

39
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:44,300
to data might be one to many.

40
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:46,980
It might be stochastic, so it's more like a stochastic inverse,

41
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:49,520
but they kind of operate in opposite directions.

42
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:51,280
And in this lecture, we're going to see

43
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:57,655
a model that explicitly models both directions simultaneously.

44
00:01:57,655 --> 00:01:59,280
So representation learning mapping data

45
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,600
to abstract representations, generative modeling,

46
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:03,120
mapping abstract representations,

47
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:06,240
like a text instruction for example to data,

48
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,280
so make an image from some text.

49
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:15,000
So the perspective that we're going to look at today

50
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,440
is going to be generative models as transformations

51
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,160
from one probability distribution

52
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,600
to another probability distribution.

53
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:27,540
So we'll have a prior distribution,

54
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:32,800
which will be the noise that we talked about last class.

55
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,348
So the prior distribution is usually

56
00:02:34,348 --> 00:02:36,140
something that is very simple in structure.

57
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:38,057
And we're going to map that prior distribution

58
00:02:38,057 --> 00:02:41,420
to a complicated data distribution.

59
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,360
So we'll always have our latent variables

60
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:46,240
or our noise space z, which is often going to be Gaussian,

61
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:48,720
indicated by a cartoon here as a circle

62
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:51,120
because it's something that is meant to be simple.

63
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,560
If it's actually a normal distribution, then

64
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,950
in high dimensions, that will be roughly a soap bubble.

65
00:02:56,950 --> 00:02:58,200
That's the way to think of it.

66
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,600
Most of the probability density of a Gaussian in high dimensions

67
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,038
is right near the surface of the hypersphere,

68
00:03:04,038 --> 00:03:06,580
and the data distribution will be something more complicated.

69
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:08,497
And so we're going to find a mapping from data

70
00:03:08,497 --> 00:03:09,740
that lives in this space.

71
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,720
And according to this distribution,

72
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:14,493
we're going to geometrically push and pull it around,

73
00:03:14,493 --> 00:03:16,160
which is what the layers of a neural net

74
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:19,080
do until we get it into this shape.

75
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:21,720
So remember those diagrams I showed before

76
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,580
of the distribution of input data points,

77
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:25,288
going layer by layer and getting remapped

78
00:03:25,288 --> 00:03:26,600
into a new distribution.

79
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,440
This is like a cartoon version of that diagram.

80
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,540
So data is x.

81
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,747
Latent variables are z.

82
00:03:34,747 --> 00:03:36,580
And in the representation learning lectures,

83
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:37,788
we looked at this direction--

84
00:03:37,788 --> 00:03:39,280
I think I used the same cartoons.

85
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:42,960
So we had a mapping f, which we called the encoder from data

86
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:44,572
to representations.

87
00:03:44,572 --> 00:03:46,280
And in the generative modeling direction,

88
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:50,200
it will be a mapping from representations to data.

89
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:55,370
And we'll call the function that generates the data a generator

90
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:57,130
g.

91
00:03:57,130 --> 00:04:03,210
So g is a mapping from z to x And f is a mapping from x to z.

92
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:05,248
So these are sometimes called generators.

93
00:04:05,248 --> 00:04:06,790
Other times, they're called decoders.

94
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:09,570
So we have an encoder that maps data

95
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:12,450
to representation a decoder that maps representation to data.

96
00:04:15,058 --> 00:04:16,350
So we've seen that a few times.

97
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:18,017
Now let's put these two things together.

98
00:04:18,017 --> 00:04:21,930
It looks like they're basically solving related problems.

99
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:24,130
And one model that puts these two things together

100
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:25,470
is, of course, the autoencoder.

101
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:27,590
Maybe you already kind of noticed that.

102
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:30,630
So an autoencoder maps from data to some vector space,

103
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:32,650
low-dimensional representation z.

104
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:36,290
And then it decodes that vector back into the data in such a way

105
00:04:36,290 --> 00:04:39,850
that you can reconstruct the data itself.

106
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:43,730
So the second half of the autoencoder, the decoder,

107
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:46,210
it's doing functionally basically what

108
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:49,610
we've described a generator, a generative model, as doing.

109
00:04:49,610 --> 00:04:53,850
So the second half of the autoencoder, the decoder,

110
00:04:53,850 --> 00:04:59,330
is a mapping from some z vector to data.

111
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:02,570
And now the question is, can we use that decoder

112
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:04,410
as a generative model, as a generator,

113
00:05:04,410 --> 00:05:07,950
to sample new random photos?

114
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:09,850
In order to do that, we need to be

115
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:12,610
able to first sample a z vector and then

116
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:15,130
decode it into an image.

117
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:18,290
So the first question for you to think about--

118
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:22,387
what is the issue with this formulation?

119
00:05:22,387 --> 00:05:22,970
That's better.

120
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:24,770
I don't know why that happened.

121
00:05:24,770 --> 00:05:30,090
So we have a mapping from z to data X.

122
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:32,750
And to sample a new random photo from the decoder,

123
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,730
we would first have to sample a new random z vector.

124
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:38,370
So what's the difficulty here?

125
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,610
How would I do that?

126
00:05:39,610 --> 00:05:41,130
Anyone can think about what would

127
00:05:41,130 --> 00:05:44,830
be difficult about sampling from some distribution over z.

128
00:05:47,770 --> 00:05:49,530
Yeah, let's go here.

129
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:50,830
AUDIENCE: What distribution?

130
00:05:50,830 --> 00:05:51,650
PHILLIP ISOLA: What distribution?

131
00:05:51,650 --> 00:05:52,990
I never told you what z is.

132
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:55,690
Yeah, in the back, I think I saw a hand.

133
00:05:55,690 --> 00:05:58,450
Right, how do you know what is the distribution

134
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:01,450
over the encodings of an autoencoder?

135
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:02,550
So that's the problem.

136
00:06:02,550 --> 00:06:05,130
So typically, an autoencoder will learn a mapping

137
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:08,270
to some distribution, some set of embeddings.

138
00:06:08,270 --> 00:06:11,850
But there's no guarantee that set of embeddings

139
00:06:11,850 --> 00:06:13,690
is going to form a simpler shape or easier

140
00:06:13,690 --> 00:06:18,570
to sample from distribution than the original data itself.

141
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:24,050
So the problem is, I can decode z vectors that

142
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:26,950
are corresponding to the data that I trained on.

143
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:30,190
So if I have f of x, where x is a real data point,

144
00:06:30,190 --> 00:06:33,050
I can decode that by doing g of f of x.

145
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:36,290
But I don't know how to sample new valid f's of x.

146
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:40,030
It might be some weird distribution full of holes.

147
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:43,530
And if I go to a point that has never been trained,

148
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:45,610
it's never been observed via f of x,

149
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:48,570
then I won't know how to decode that.

150
00:06:48,570 --> 00:06:51,010
So the whole point of a variational autoencoder

151
00:06:51,010 --> 00:06:54,410
is to fix this problem is to make it so that the latent

152
00:06:54,410 --> 00:06:56,290
space, the encodings of an autoencoder,

153
00:06:56,290 --> 00:06:58,710
form a simple distribution that you can sample from.

154
00:06:58,710 --> 00:07:01,170
That's all of the math and algorithms

155
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:04,010
that we'll develop in this lecture are going to be simply

156
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:07,090
to do that, simply to make it so that this becomes

157
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:10,570
a sample from distribution.

158
00:07:10,570 --> 00:07:15,170
So we're going to go through VAEs

159
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:16,590
from a few different perspectives.

160
00:07:16,590 --> 00:07:21,558
We're going to build up the kind of mechanistic intuition

161
00:07:21,558 --> 00:07:22,350
of what's going on.

162
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:24,142
But we'll also look at the theoretical kind

163
00:07:24,142 --> 00:07:28,010
of probabilistic modeling way of motivating these things,

164
00:07:28,010 --> 00:07:33,530
and try to understand at least some of the layers of analysis

165
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:34,613
that I'm going to provide.

166
00:07:34,613 --> 00:07:36,822
So it might be that you get lost in some of the math,

167
00:07:36,822 --> 00:07:38,630
but then always remember to pop back out.

168
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:40,672
So the first perspective I want you to understand

169
00:07:40,672 --> 00:07:45,210
is that a VAE is an autoencoder with one change, which

170
00:07:45,210 --> 00:07:49,020
is that rather than the encoder mapping to a complicated shape,

171
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:50,270
it will map to a simple shape.

172
00:07:50,270 --> 00:07:52,230
It will map to a Gaussian.

173
00:07:52,230 --> 00:07:54,130
So a VAE is just an autoencoder that

174
00:07:54,130 --> 00:07:57,290
maps where f maps to a Gaussian, and that

175
00:07:57,290 --> 00:07:59,490
means that I can sample new images by sampling

176
00:07:59,490 --> 00:08:01,930
from a Gaussian and running the Gaussian

177
00:08:01,930 --> 00:08:03,630
random vectors through decoder.

178
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:06,030
That's the first level of understanding of a VAE.

179
00:08:09,050 --> 00:08:09,930
Question?

180
00:08:09,930 --> 00:08:13,770
AUDIENCE: Is the reason that we want the latent space to be

181
00:08:13,770 --> 00:08:15,730
Gaussian specifically because it makes

182
00:08:15,730 --> 00:08:17,910
it better to generate models?

183
00:08:17,910 --> 00:08:20,050
Or is this an intrinsic good [? of having the ?]

184
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:21,730
representation space or something?

185
00:08:21,730 --> 00:08:23,730
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yes, so why do we want the latent

186
00:08:23,730 --> 00:08:24,872
space to be Gaussian?

187
00:08:24,872 --> 00:08:27,330
So the level of understanding that I want you to have right

188
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:30,350
now is that if the latent space were Gaussian,

189
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:32,370
you could sample from it, and then it becomes

190
00:08:32,370 --> 00:08:33,630
a generative model sample.

191
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:37,450
A Gaussian from z, decode, becomes an image.

192
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:43,730
Now you also asked, is it that a Gaussian is a better--

193
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:45,730
that leads to better representations?

194
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:48,313
If I just want to think of this from a representation learning

195
00:08:48,313 --> 00:08:51,350
angle, is mapping to a Gaussian distribution a good idea?

196
00:08:51,350 --> 00:08:54,670
And that's also true, but it's a little bit more subtle.

197
00:08:54,670 --> 00:08:58,790
So we'll get to that toward the end.

198
00:08:58,790 --> 00:09:01,670
So VAEs are great representation learners and great generative

199
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:03,670
models, or they're good at both of those things.

200
00:09:03,670 --> 00:09:06,170
That's why we're getting to them after we've already

201
00:09:06,170 --> 00:09:09,490
covered basic representation learning and basic generative

202
00:09:09,490 --> 00:09:12,330
modeling because the VAE puts both of them together.

203
00:09:12,330 --> 00:09:15,435
And it encompasses all the models we've seen.

204
00:09:15,435 --> 00:09:17,810
And many of the models we've seen, like diffusion models,

205
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:20,770
can be cast as a certain kind of VAE

206
00:09:20,770 --> 00:09:22,790
or autoencoders or a degenerate VAE.

207
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:24,210
So VAEs are kind of--

208
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:26,090
they're the pinnacle of generative modeling

209
00:09:26,090 --> 00:09:28,810
and representation learning.

210
00:09:28,810 --> 00:09:32,430
But in practice, they're not as popular

211
00:09:32,430 --> 00:09:34,430
these days as diffusion models and other things.

212
00:09:34,430 --> 00:09:36,055
But mathematically, they're a framework

213
00:09:36,055 --> 00:09:39,470
that encompasses almost everything that we've seen.

214
00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:44,405
So what is the probability interpretation of a VAE?

215
00:09:44,405 --> 00:09:46,030
So now we're going to take a step back,

216
00:09:46,030 --> 00:09:47,910
and we're going to talk about mixtures of Gaussians

217
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:49,730
because it turns out, variational autoencoders

218
00:09:49,730 --> 00:09:51,147
are fitting a mixture of Gaussians

219
00:09:51,147 --> 00:09:53,008
to a data distribution.

220
00:09:53,008 --> 00:09:55,550
So a mixture of Gaussians-- so what is a mixture of Gaussian?

221
00:09:55,550 --> 00:09:57,050
Well, we talked about--

222
00:09:57,050 --> 00:09:58,430
we've seen Gaussians.

223
00:09:58,430 --> 00:10:01,930
So a Gaussian fit to data with a max

224
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:03,690
likelihood objective is just going

225
00:10:03,690 --> 00:10:06,250
to be a density model where we try

226
00:10:06,250 --> 00:10:08,990
to increase the density that we apply to where the data lives.

227
00:10:08,990 --> 00:10:11,888
So there'll be a gradient that pushes up the density here.

228
00:10:11,888 --> 00:10:13,430
And the parameters are just the mean.

229
00:10:13,430 --> 00:10:15,930
And the variance will shift around the mean and the variance

230
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:18,410
of this Gaussian until we get maximum likelihood applied

231
00:10:18,410 --> 00:10:19,230
to the data points.

232
00:10:19,230 --> 00:10:22,410
And that's what we learned about last Thursday.

233
00:10:22,410 --> 00:10:24,130
So that's one Gaussian.

234
00:10:24,130 --> 00:10:26,410
A mixture of Gaussians is just, well,

235
00:10:26,410 --> 00:10:29,930
in order to model a complicated distribution that does not

236
00:10:29,930 --> 00:10:32,090
have a Gaussian shape, I can use a bunch

237
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:35,130
of Gaussian distributions and sum them up.

238
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:37,435
So a Gaussian is like a little-- think of it

239
00:10:37,435 --> 00:10:38,310
like a little circle.

240
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:40,643
I mean, it could be an ellipse, but most of this lecture

241
00:10:40,643 --> 00:10:42,760
will just assume isotropic Gaussians,

242
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:46,800
which means that the covariance is 1 or is the identity,

243
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:47,860
in fact.

244
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:50,540
So we're going to assume, just for simplicity,

245
00:10:50,540 --> 00:10:53,160
that there's no ellipsoids or covariance structure.

246
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,500
It's just circles.

247
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:58,860
You can always turn these into ellipses, but in the pictures,

248
00:10:58,860 --> 00:10:59,880
I'll just draw circles.

249
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:02,540
So what does it look like to have a mixture of Gaussians that

250
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:04,780
is fit to some distribution, like this funny shape

251
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:07,040
that I was coloring in blue on the previous slides?

252
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:08,040
It just looks like this.

253
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,020
I'm going to say I can approximate that shape

254
00:11:10,020 --> 00:11:11,503
with a bunch of circles.

255
00:11:11,503 --> 00:11:13,920
If the circles are small enough and I have enough of them,

256
00:11:13,920 --> 00:11:16,980
then I can approximate any shape.

257
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:19,210
So probably, you could prove--

258
00:11:19,210 --> 00:11:21,460
I haven't thought through this, but probably a mixture

259
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:23,620
of Gaussians can, to arbitrary precision,

260
00:11:23,620 --> 00:11:27,560
approximate any density, something like that.

261
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:29,680
So here's what the mathematical model is.

262
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:35,380
It's just a mixture, so a sum, with weightings w.

263
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:37,725
I think, in traditional models, these would be positive.

264
00:11:37,725 --> 00:11:40,100
But you can imagine these being negative or positive more

265
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:41,340
generally.

266
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:45,580
And every component is going to be a Gaussian, a little blip

267
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:48,820
of Gaussian probability centered at a mean, mu,

268
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:51,940
and covariance, sigma.

269
00:11:51,940 --> 00:11:55,060
So the mean of the first mixture component

270
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:57,940
is here and the covariance is--

271
00:11:57,940 --> 00:11:59,660
in this case, it's just isotropic two

272
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:00,760
dimensional Gaussian.

273
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,140
So we're just going to have a covariance

274
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:04,475
is equal to the variance.

275
00:12:04,475 --> 00:12:06,600
It's just one number that characterizes that shape.

276
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:10,100
It's the radius of that circle.

277
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:12,380
So that's the mixture of Gaussians.

278
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:14,300
And the parameters of the mixture of Gaussians

279
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:17,980
are the mixing weights, w, the means

280
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:20,300
and the variances, the covariances

281
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:22,563
of all of the Gaussians in the mixture.

282
00:12:22,563 --> 00:12:23,980
And in general, in high dimensions

283
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,748
the covariance will be d by d dimensional matrix,

284
00:12:27,748 --> 00:12:29,540
where d is the dimensionality, and the mean

285
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:32,060
will be a d-dimensional vector.

286
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:36,385
So VAEs are a mixture of Gaussians.

287
00:12:36,385 --> 00:12:37,760
But they're not just any mixture.

288
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:40,340
They're actually an infinite mixture of Gaussians.

289
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:42,600
So mixture of Gaussians is a classical model.

290
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:43,980
That kind of new thing with a VAE

291
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:47,180
is it's an infinite mixture of Gaussians.

292
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:53,560
So how could I parameterize an infinite mixture of Gaussians?

293
00:12:53,560 --> 00:12:55,820
So a mixture of Gaussians--

294
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:58,980
I have weight, so that's one parameter.

295
00:12:58,980 --> 00:12:59,580
I have mean.

296
00:12:59,580 --> 00:13:00,500
That's d parameters.

297
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,443
And covariance-- that's d squared parameters.

298
00:13:02,443 --> 00:13:03,360
And I have k of those.

299
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:07,780
So I have d squared times k plus times k plus parameters

300
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:11,140
to specify k Gaussians.

301
00:13:11,140 --> 00:13:12,820
So to specify infinite Gaussians,

302
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:15,820
I need infinite parameters.

303
00:13:15,820 --> 00:13:17,880
So clearly, that's not going to work,

304
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:21,900
that explicit parameterization of infinite mixture components.

305
00:13:21,900 --> 00:13:25,780
So there's a really cool trick for parameterizing

306
00:13:25,780 --> 00:13:29,220
an infinite mixture with a finite number of parameters.

307
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:31,700
And that is to parameterize that infinite mixture

308
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:34,040
as a mapping from some underlying base

309
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:40,300
continuous distribution to mixture components via function

310
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:42,820
g that has a finite set of parameters.

311
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:46,380
And in this course, g will be a neural network.

312
00:13:46,380 --> 00:13:49,180
But in fact, a VAE doesn't have to have anything

313
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:52,220
to do with neural networks. g could be any type of mapping.

314
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:55,500
But in this course and in traditional literature,

315
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:58,180
VAEs are always using neural nets.

316
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:03,882
But yeah, really, you could apply this to other models, too.

317
00:14:03,882 --> 00:14:04,840
So here's how it works.

318
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:05,600
We have pz.

319
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:07,900
This is a unit Gaussian distribution.

320
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:11,420
We sample a random vector from that space.

321
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:16,220
We pass it through our generator or decoder g.

322
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:18,860
And that outputs a number, which we

323
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:23,220
will interpret as the mean of one of my infinite mixture

324
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:24,580
components.

325
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,660
So I'll index the mean with this symbol mu,

326
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:31,020
and we'll have the neural network g or the function g

327
00:14:31,020 --> 00:14:32,760
actually also output the variance.

328
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:34,380
So it'll output a set of numbers,

329
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:37,500
a d, a d-dimensional vector for the mean,

330
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:41,740
and a d-squared-dimensional vector for the covariance

331
00:14:41,740 --> 00:14:44,740
or, in this example, two numbers for the mean and maybe one

332
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:47,140
number to characterize the covariance because it's

333
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:50,500
an isotropic Gaussian.

334
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:54,120
So if I sample different points in the z space,

335
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:58,400
I will end up parameterizing different Gaussians.

336
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:01,940
And because z is continuous, I can sample any point

337
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:04,740
on a continuum, and I can thereby

338
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:07,380
get an infinite number of possible or continuous

339
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:12,060
possible set of Gaussian mixture components.

340
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:14,560
And g just has weights and biases.

341
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:15,780
It's a neural net.

342
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:18,780
So that's a finite number of parameters

343
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:21,660
to model an infinite mixture.

344
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:28,660
So this has parameters theta.

345
00:15:28,660 --> 00:15:33,780
And now we have fit some weird complicated distribution

346
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:37,660
with an infinite mixture parameterized by a neural net

347
00:15:37,660 --> 00:15:39,560
with weights and biases theta.

348
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:47,300
So what is the density implied by this mapping?

349
00:15:47,300 --> 00:15:50,140
So it's going to be not a sum because we

350
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:51,400
have a continuous space.

351
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:53,260
So we have to switch to a continuous math

352
00:15:53,260 --> 00:15:57,980
and use integrals, so the probability of some data point

353
00:15:57,980 --> 00:15:58,480
x.

354
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:02,500
So the distribution over x implied by this model

355
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:06,640
is going to be an integral over all of my mixture components.

356
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,140
Each mixture component is just going

357
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:10,580
to be a normal distribution, a Gaussian,

358
00:16:10,580 --> 00:16:12,820
with mean given by the neural net

359
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:15,140
and variance given by the neural net.

360
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:18,220
That will place a little blip of density over x.

361
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:24,200
And my total probability will just be marginalizing over z,

362
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:25,200
so integrating over z.

363
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:27,200
So here's the finite set of Gaussian.

364
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:29,320
It's the sum over my mixture components.

365
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:31,860
But now I have an infinite set of mixture components.

366
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:33,980
And I'm going to take the integral

367
00:16:33,980 --> 00:16:35,480
over all of those components.

368
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:37,560
And that integral is over z space.

369
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:44,580
So this is the infinite mixture of Gaussian model.

370
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:47,560
These are the weights. pz is my prior distribution.

371
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:49,360
It's usually just a unit Gaussian.

372
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,740
And think of that the weights of the mixture components.

373
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:53,657
And then these are all the mixture components.

374
00:16:53,657 --> 00:16:55,020
And it's an integral.

375
00:16:55,020 --> 00:16:58,980
Any questions about that?

376
00:16:58,980 --> 00:17:01,020
So this is the probability model that underlies

377
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:03,420
VAEs for a Gaussian VAE.

378
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:05,065
You can actually have non-Gaussian.

379
00:17:05,065 --> 00:17:06,440
So you could have other mixtures.

380
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:08,315
It doesn't have to be a mixture of Gaussians.

381
00:17:08,315 --> 00:17:10,807
It could be a mixture of Laplace distributions

382
00:17:10,807 --> 00:17:12,640
or Poisson distributions, whatever you want.

383
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:14,098
So it doesn't have to be Gaussians.

384
00:17:14,098 --> 00:17:16,060
But usually, we'll work with Gaussians.

385
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:18,435
AUDIENCE: Is this a convolution, or is that the wrong way

386
00:17:18,435 --> 00:17:19,060
to look at it?

387
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:20,435
PHILLIP ISOLA: Is it convolution?

388
00:17:20,435 --> 00:17:24,619
I suppose it could be interpreted as convolution.

389
00:17:24,619 --> 00:17:27,880
Like you're convolving over z-space with--

390
00:17:32,377 --> 00:17:34,710
yeah, I'm not sure that's the most useful way of looking

391
00:17:34,710 --> 00:17:36,252
at it, but you could probably cast it

392
00:17:36,252 --> 00:17:38,030
as convolution in some sense.

393
00:17:38,030 --> 00:17:38,910
Yeah.

394
00:17:38,910 --> 00:17:42,030
OK, so I think it's easier to think

395
00:17:42,030 --> 00:17:45,350
of it as marginal likelihood and marginalizing over some latent

396
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:47,830
variables, so that's what we'll see on this slide.

397
00:17:47,830 --> 00:17:50,390
So we're going to understand the z-variable--

398
00:17:50,390 --> 00:17:52,510
the z-space as being latent variables that

399
00:17:52,510 --> 00:17:55,670
describe all the attributes of the generated data,

400
00:17:55,670 --> 00:18:01,010
but in an abstract fashion, and all those latent variables,

401
00:18:01,010 --> 00:18:03,670
we'll hope that they have a simple distribution.

402
00:18:03,670 --> 00:18:08,070
And they'll be unit Gaussians in the VAE, but in the example

403
00:18:08,070 --> 00:18:09,950
I gave last class, I said that I could

404
00:18:09,950 --> 00:18:11,750
write a Python program that will take noise

405
00:18:11,750 --> 00:18:14,130
from simple distributions and generate images,

406
00:18:14,130 --> 00:18:16,110
and these simple distributions are

407
00:18:16,110 --> 00:18:17,930
my distribution over the latent variables,

408
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:20,070
and they have interpretations.

409
00:18:20,070 --> 00:18:22,705
So the graphical model-- if you've

410
00:18:22,705 --> 00:18:25,330
done probability with graphical models it would look like this.

411
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:27,270
We sample a z, and then condition on the z.

412
00:18:27,270 --> 00:18:29,787
We sample an x, we only observe the x.

413
00:18:29,787 --> 00:18:31,370
The latent variables are not observed.

414
00:18:31,370 --> 00:18:34,790
Our data modeling problem is to fit a distribution

415
00:18:34,790 --> 00:18:40,430
over x that is represented as a mapping from some underlying

416
00:18:40,430 --> 00:18:41,750
variable z.

417
00:18:41,750 --> 00:18:42,770
But we don't observe z.

418
00:18:42,770 --> 00:18:45,350
We have to maybe infer z from x.

419
00:18:45,350 --> 00:18:46,110
OK.

420
00:18:46,110 --> 00:18:48,150
So the generator will be-- think of that

421
00:18:48,150 --> 00:18:50,750
as a conditional distribution, the probability over x

422
00:18:50,750 --> 00:18:54,110
given some setting of the latent variables.

423
00:18:54,110 --> 00:19:04,110
And if I want to model the likelihood of a data point x,

424
00:19:04,110 --> 00:19:07,750
I can integrate over my latent variables z.

425
00:19:07,750 --> 00:19:11,190
So this is just marginalization from probability.

426
00:19:11,190 --> 00:19:14,170
Px given z times pz is px and z.

427
00:19:14,170 --> 00:19:18,150
Then if I integrate over z, then by the rules of probability,

428
00:19:18,150 --> 00:19:20,590
I get px.

429
00:19:20,590 --> 00:19:21,090
OK.

430
00:19:21,090 --> 00:19:23,330
So this is called-- this is-- the likelihood

431
00:19:23,330 --> 00:19:26,163
the probability that the model p theta places on the data

432
00:19:26,163 --> 00:19:27,830
is called the likelihood, and I'm trying

433
00:19:27,830 --> 00:19:29,050
to do max likelihood learning.

434
00:19:29,050 --> 00:19:30,758
So I want to find the probability model p

435
00:19:30,758 --> 00:19:34,070
theta that maximizes the likelihood of the data that

436
00:19:34,070 --> 00:19:36,230
assigns the most density to the data.

437
00:19:36,230 --> 00:19:37,950
And to compute the likelihood, I have

438
00:19:37,950 --> 00:19:41,030
to marginalize over my unobserved latent variables.

439
00:19:41,030 --> 00:19:43,648
So sometimes we call this marginal likelihood.

440
00:19:43,648 --> 00:19:45,190
So a latent variable model, I'm going

441
00:19:45,190 --> 00:19:48,430
to fit it to data by maximizing the marginal likelihood that it

442
00:19:48,430 --> 00:19:50,750
assigns to data when I marginalize over all the latent

443
00:19:50,750 --> 00:19:52,310
variables.

444
00:19:52,310 --> 00:19:54,310
Because for a given data point, there

445
00:19:54,310 --> 00:19:56,657
might-- to make it high probability,

446
00:19:56,657 --> 00:19:58,990
there might be a lot of different settings of the latent

447
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:02,190
variable that can give it some plausible-- like,

448
00:20:02,190 --> 00:20:04,430
a high probability.

449
00:20:04,430 --> 00:20:06,830
OK.

450
00:20:06,830 --> 00:20:10,510
OK, so here is the entire objective function

451
00:20:10,510 --> 00:20:13,630
and hypothesis space of a variational autoencoder,

452
00:20:13,630 --> 00:20:17,930
or in this case, just an infinite Gaussian mixture model,

453
00:20:17,930 --> 00:20:20,350
and the variational autoencoder can be--

454
00:20:20,350 --> 00:20:23,730
is one type of approach that uses this model.

455
00:20:23,730 --> 00:20:27,230
So the objective function is going

456
00:20:27,230 --> 00:20:30,170
to be to do maximum likelihood.

457
00:20:30,170 --> 00:20:32,710
So I'm going to maximize the likelihood that my model places

458
00:20:32,710 --> 00:20:34,870
on the data.

459
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:36,665
And the hypothesis space is going

460
00:20:36,665 --> 00:20:38,290
to be an infinite mixture of Gaussians.

461
00:20:38,290 --> 00:20:43,110
So I'm going to try to find the means and the variances

462
00:20:43,110 --> 00:20:46,650
and the weights of Gaussians in an infinite mixture,

463
00:20:46,650 --> 00:20:48,330
and to parameterize an infinite mixture,

464
00:20:48,330 --> 00:20:51,790
I'm going to parameterize a mapping

465
00:20:51,790 --> 00:20:58,350
from some underlying prior space z to the data space x

466
00:20:58,350 --> 00:21:01,950
via the math that we just looked at on the last few slides.

467
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:03,110
OK.

468
00:21:03,110 --> 00:21:04,530
So this is marginal likelihood.

469
00:21:04,530 --> 00:21:06,830
I'm marginalizing over the latent variables z.

470
00:21:06,830 --> 00:21:12,010
And the likelihood term for px given z is going to be Gaussian,

471
00:21:12,010 --> 00:21:15,910
and the prior term for pz is going to be unit Gaussian,

472
00:21:15,910 --> 00:21:18,310
mapping from unit Gaussian to complicated data

473
00:21:18,310 --> 00:21:21,870
distribution in that fashion.

474
00:21:21,870 --> 00:21:24,130
And this model is going to give me two things.

475
00:21:24,130 --> 00:21:28,250
It's going to give me both a density-- that's the main thing.

476
00:21:28,250 --> 00:21:30,710
I'm trying to learn the parameters theta that

477
00:21:30,710 --> 00:21:33,990
will define this density function that maps from x

478
00:21:33,990 --> 00:21:36,670
to non-negative numbers.

479
00:21:36,670 --> 00:21:38,670
And it will actually also give me

480
00:21:38,670 --> 00:21:41,410
a very easy way of sampling from that density.

481
00:21:41,410 --> 00:21:43,110
So it gives me those two things.

482
00:21:43,110 --> 00:21:48,130
So in order to sample from this density, I can do the following.

483
00:21:48,130 --> 00:21:51,150
I first sample a random unit Gaussian.

484
00:21:51,150 --> 00:21:55,590
So that's sampling my z vector, And then I decode it through g.

485
00:21:55,590 --> 00:21:56,130
OK.

486
00:21:56,130 --> 00:21:58,550
So in order to create a sample x,

487
00:21:58,550 --> 00:22:03,830
I first sample a random unit Gaussian z.

488
00:22:03,830 --> 00:22:05,710
Or, in fact, this sampler that's written here

489
00:22:05,710 --> 00:22:10,890
is just going to be the mapping p of x given z.

490
00:22:10,890 --> 00:22:13,870
So it's going to say given some z, how

491
00:22:13,870 --> 00:22:18,590
do I draw a sample over the possible x's that

492
00:22:18,590 --> 00:22:21,230
follow the density px given z?

493
00:22:21,230 --> 00:22:28,750
So I will take my z, and I will have my neural network predict

494
00:22:28,750 --> 00:22:31,390
what is the mean of the mixture component,

495
00:22:31,390 --> 00:22:35,350
and then I will transform--

496
00:22:35,350 --> 00:22:38,390
I will take a sample with some variance

497
00:22:38,390 --> 00:22:42,490
by multiplying my variance by a unit Gaussian.

498
00:22:42,490 --> 00:22:46,150
So this is just a way to sample from a Gaussian

499
00:22:46,150 --> 00:22:48,830
whose mean is g of mu and whose variance

500
00:22:48,830 --> 00:22:53,590
is g sigma by transforming a sample from a unit Gaussian.

501
00:22:56,110 --> 00:22:58,750
So x will be distributed as normal distribution

502
00:22:58,750 --> 00:23:05,310
whose mean is g of mu and whose variance is g of sigma.

503
00:23:05,310 --> 00:23:07,100
OK.

504
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:10,830
So the difficulty here--

505
00:23:10,830 --> 00:23:11,410
let's see.

506
00:23:14,490 --> 00:23:14,990
Sorry.

507
00:23:14,990 --> 00:23:17,030
The variance is sigma squared.

508
00:23:17,030 --> 00:23:20,290
I suppose sigma is the standard deviation, not the variance.

509
00:23:20,290 --> 00:23:22,090
I always mix up those two.

510
00:23:22,090 --> 00:23:23,830
OK.

511
00:23:23,830 --> 00:23:27,070
So, yeah, the variance--

512
00:23:27,070 --> 00:23:28,750
did I get this right?

513
00:23:28,750 --> 00:23:31,090
Variance is square of standard deviation.

514
00:23:31,090 --> 00:23:32,110
OK, yeah.

515
00:23:32,110 --> 00:23:38,690
So the difficulty here is this term right here.

516
00:23:38,690 --> 00:23:41,750
So if I want to maximize the likelihood that my model assigns

517
00:23:41,750 --> 00:23:43,830
to the data, well, the likelihood

518
00:23:43,830 --> 00:23:45,670
is written as an integral.

519
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:48,550
So if I were to try to solve this with back-propagation

520
00:23:48,550 --> 00:23:51,750
or something like that, that means that every time I'm

521
00:23:51,750 --> 00:23:54,430
going to perturb my parameters to place more probability, more

522
00:23:54,430 --> 00:23:58,550
likelihood on the data, I would have to compute this integral.

523
00:23:58,550 --> 00:24:01,990
And this would be incredibly expensive.

524
00:24:01,990 --> 00:24:05,690
So we don't even have a closed form for that integral,

525
00:24:05,690 --> 00:24:09,110
so we would compute that integral by approximating it

526
00:24:09,110 --> 00:24:10,710
with a set of samples by approximating

527
00:24:10,710 --> 00:24:11,810
the integral somehow.

528
00:24:11,810 --> 00:24:13,650
To get a good approximation to an integral,

529
00:24:13,650 --> 00:24:17,370
I need to maybe take a lot of samples over z.

530
00:24:17,370 --> 00:24:21,640
And this means that every time I'm

531
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:25,040
going to run my model forward, see

532
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:26,940
what density it places in my data,

533
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:29,382
I actually have to run it forward a lot, a lot of times

534
00:24:29,382 --> 00:24:30,840
because look at the neural network,

535
00:24:30,840 --> 00:24:32,660
and g appears inside the integral,

536
00:24:32,660 --> 00:24:35,480
so to get a good approximation to the likelihood

537
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:37,560
my model places on the data, I have

538
00:24:37,560 --> 00:24:42,160
to run my neural network forward as many times as necessary

539
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:45,480
to approximate this integral, which might be a lot of times.

540
00:24:45,480 --> 00:24:48,680
So one step of backprop is running my neural network

541
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:51,600
forward many, many, many times to get

542
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:53,780
a good estimate of this integral,

543
00:24:53,780 --> 00:24:55,880
and then I still have to backprop that.

544
00:24:55,880 --> 00:24:56,380
OK.

545
00:24:56,380 --> 00:24:59,460
So this integral is difficult to calculate.

546
00:24:59,460 --> 00:25:03,680
It's an intractable integral, so what am I going to do?

547
00:25:03,680 --> 00:25:04,360
Oh.

548
00:25:04,360 --> 00:25:06,560
And just notation-wise, there's something

549
00:25:06,560 --> 00:25:09,365
that you might encounter in the literature, which

550
00:25:09,365 --> 00:25:11,240
is called the reparameterization trick, which

551
00:25:11,240 --> 00:25:12,320
just refers to this.

552
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:18,120
In order to take a sample from this distribution, px given

553
00:25:18,120 --> 00:25:21,400
z, which is a normal distribution,

554
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:23,720
it suffices to take a sample from a unit Gaussian

555
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:25,800
and transform it by offsetting it by the mean

556
00:25:25,800 --> 00:25:29,000
and then multiplying it by the variance.

557
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:30,880
OK.

558
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:34,600
So then x is just a deterministic function

559
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:37,360
of g with the only stochasticity coming

560
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:41,700
in through this unit Gaussian noise inserted in the side.

561
00:25:41,700 --> 00:25:43,320
It's just a little trick.

562
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:44,560
AUDIENCE: Just to clarify.

563
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:48,560
So you say a variance because only sigma squared, is it

564
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:49,580
standard deviation?

565
00:25:49,580 --> 00:25:50,440
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

566
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:54,960
Sorry, I got mixed up in the terminology.

567
00:25:54,960 --> 00:25:57,980
So sigma is the standard deviation,

568
00:25:57,980 --> 00:25:59,500
sigma squared is the variance.

569
00:25:59,500 --> 00:26:01,780
And, yeah, apologies for mixing up those terms.

570
00:26:01,780 --> 00:26:03,040
AUDIENCE: So is this--

571
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:05,620
it should be standard deviation or is this a typo,

572
00:26:05,620 --> 00:26:07,600
it should be sigma squared?

573
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:09,760
PHILLIP ISOLA: No, I believe that it

574
00:26:09,760 --> 00:26:13,520
should be multiplied by the standard deviation, yeah.

575
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:15,660
I think this is not a typo, it's just I misspoke.

576
00:26:18,180 --> 00:26:18,680
OK.

577
00:26:18,680 --> 00:26:19,722
Anybody can confirm that?

578
00:26:19,722 --> 00:26:22,040
That's the proper way to transform a unit Gaussian

579
00:26:22,040 --> 00:26:24,100
into a Gaussian with some variance?

580
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:24,900
OK, good.

581
00:26:24,900 --> 00:26:26,760
So you multiply by the standard deviation.

582
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:28,320
Square root of the variance.

583
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:30,440
OK.

584
00:26:30,440 --> 00:26:31,480
OK.

585
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:35,580
So that's the problem that we're trying to solve,

586
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:39,160
and the rest of the VAE trickery is going to be,

587
00:26:39,160 --> 00:26:43,440
how do you calculate this integral in an efficient manner?

588
00:26:43,440 --> 00:26:45,640
So you could pause here and you could say you

589
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:47,820
understand what VAEs are doing.

590
00:26:47,820 --> 00:26:52,520
They're modeling the-- they're maximizing the data likelihood

591
00:26:52,520 --> 00:26:55,800
by fitting an infinite mixture of Gaussians to the data

592
00:26:55,800 --> 00:26:59,280
and doing optimization to increase the probability we

593
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:01,680
place on x with respect to the parameters theta, which

594
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:04,443
are the weights and biases of the neural network g.

595
00:27:04,443 --> 00:27:05,860
That's one layer of understanding.

596
00:27:05,860 --> 00:27:08,318
But then there's this integral-- so the rest of the lecture

597
00:27:08,318 --> 00:27:12,320
is actually how do we approximate that integral.

598
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:14,560
OK.

599
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:16,880
OK, so I'm going to use the notation

600
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:22,520
L to represent the objective function, which is the data log

601
00:27:22,520 --> 00:27:23,720
likelihood.

602
00:27:23,720 --> 00:27:27,060
So L is equal to this equation down here.

603
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:32,220
So that equation is the marginal likelihood--

604
00:27:32,220 --> 00:27:35,000
so it's going to be the probability of x given z times

605
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:37,600
z integrated over z is px.

606
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:39,732
Log of px is the log likelihood.

607
00:27:39,732 --> 00:27:41,440
I'm trying to maximize the log likelihood

608
00:27:41,440 --> 00:27:43,520
my model places on the data.

609
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:47,860
And this outer sum over n data points is going to be-- well,

610
00:27:47,860 --> 00:27:48,540
my training set.

611
00:27:48,540 --> 00:27:51,040
I'm going to be fitting my model to a training

612
00:27:51,040 --> 00:27:53,640
set of n observations.

613
00:27:53,640 --> 00:27:55,240
So maybe I have n photographs and I

614
00:27:55,240 --> 00:27:57,120
want to learn a generative model of them.

615
00:27:57,120 --> 00:28:02,000
So I-- since this is log likelihood, when

616
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:05,300
I have multiple different data points,

617
00:28:05,300 --> 00:28:08,800
I maximize the joint probability by maximizing the sum

618
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:11,660
over the log probabilities.

619
00:28:11,660 --> 00:28:14,440
OK, so this is the objective function

620
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:20,040
for maximizing the likelihood using a mixture of Gaussians.

621
00:28:20,040 --> 00:28:21,810
OK.

622
00:28:21,810 --> 00:28:24,440
So I'm now going to introduce a series of tricks--

623
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:28,020
I think around three tricks for approximating this integral.

624
00:28:28,020 --> 00:28:31,200
So the first trick is going to be the most vanilla trick

625
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:33,985
that we use all the time, which is to approximate an integral I

626
00:28:33,985 --> 00:28:34,860
can use some sample--

627
00:28:34,860 --> 00:28:36,520
I can use sampling in some form.

628
00:28:36,520 --> 00:28:38,120
And the nice thing about this integral

629
00:28:38,120 --> 00:28:41,620
is it's actually in the form of an expectation.

630
00:28:41,620 --> 00:28:44,520
So this marginal likelihood, marginalizing over the latent

631
00:28:44,520 --> 00:28:49,080
variables, is equal to the expectation

632
00:28:49,080 --> 00:28:52,360
of how much likelihood probability density

633
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:57,760
I place on x for each z in expectation over random samples

634
00:28:57,760 --> 00:29:01,360
from the prior pz.

635
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:04,140
So that's the definition of expectation.

636
00:29:04,140 --> 00:29:08,320
It's an integral with--

637
00:29:08,320 --> 00:29:10,180
if I sample from this distribution,

638
00:29:10,180 --> 00:29:13,960
it goes outside the expectation, this is just a definition.

639
00:29:13,960 --> 00:29:15,880
OK.

640
00:29:15,880 --> 00:29:19,880
So, how do we approximate expectations?

641
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:22,960
You can approximate expectations by simply taking a finite set

642
00:29:22,960 --> 00:29:26,160
of samples and averaging them.

643
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:29,160
That's the trick that comes up all the time in math.

644
00:29:29,160 --> 00:29:31,160
If I have an expectation, I approximate it

645
00:29:31,160 --> 00:29:34,800
with a Monte Carlo estimate, which is a set of samples,

646
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:36,520
and then I take the average, as opposed

647
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:39,280
to having to compute the entire integral.

648
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:42,360
As M goes to infinity, this becomes--

649
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:44,900
this is an unbiased estimator of that integral.

650
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:49,120
So as M goes to infinity, then I get a perfect estimate.

651
00:29:49,120 --> 00:29:50,860
OK, so how do I do that?

652
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:53,740
I sample from my prior, which is unit Gaussian.

653
00:29:53,740 --> 00:29:54,340
That's easy.

654
00:29:54,340 --> 00:29:57,240
We can sample unit Gaussian vectors.

655
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:01,840
And then I sum up and take the average of the density

656
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:05,300
that the model places over x for each z.

657
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:08,700
And remember that px given z is a very simple distribution.

658
00:30:08,700 --> 00:30:11,600
That's just a Gaussian distribution.

659
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:12,940
That was here.

660
00:30:12,940 --> 00:30:15,880
So px given z is just a Gaussian distribution parametrized

661
00:30:15,880 --> 00:30:19,040
by my neural net output mean and variance.

662
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:20,540
OK.

663
00:30:20,540 --> 00:30:22,540
So this is trick number one.

664
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:24,520
And now that we have done that trick,

665
00:30:24,520 --> 00:30:25,773
we actually can train this.

666
00:30:25,773 --> 00:30:27,440
I can actually write PyTorch and do this

667
00:30:27,440 --> 00:30:28,857
because I got rid of the integral,

668
00:30:28,857 --> 00:30:31,100
I don't know how to write integrals in PyTorch.

669
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:33,640
But now I've got it into a form where

670
00:30:33,640 --> 00:30:35,040
we can write it in PyTorch.

671
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:39,500
Everything is just countable, discrete math.

672
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:44,080
So I approximated the integral with an average over average

673
00:30:44,080 --> 00:30:46,633
over M samples from my prior, and I

674
00:30:46,633 --> 00:30:48,800
looked at how much probability each of those samples

675
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:49,940
places on my data.

676
00:30:49,940 --> 00:30:53,240
I add it all up.

677
00:30:53,240 --> 00:30:57,220
And then I will-- it's a log probability,

678
00:30:57,220 --> 00:31:03,080
so I'm trying to estimate log of p theta x.

679
00:31:03,080 --> 00:31:06,010
So log of-- this is p theta x.

680
00:31:06,010 --> 00:31:09,010
This is my approximation to p theta x.

681
00:31:09,010 --> 00:31:14,490
And then I will be-- my total probability over all my training

682
00:31:14,490 --> 00:31:18,530
data points is a sum over the n data points,

683
00:31:18,530 --> 00:31:20,530
and I'm going to try to find the parameters that

684
00:31:20,530 --> 00:31:25,030
maximize the probability I place over all of my data points.

685
00:31:25,030 --> 00:31:25,530
OK.

686
00:31:29,870 --> 00:31:30,370
Ah.

687
00:31:30,370 --> 00:31:31,650
I see-- I think I--

688
00:31:31,650 --> 00:31:32,150
no.

689
00:31:32,150 --> 00:31:33,067
Oh yeah, this is fine.

