00:36:03,758 --> 00:36:05,550
space-- or higher-dimensional latent space.

791
00:36:05,550 --> 00:36:09,010
So let's say that my z vectors are hundreds dimensional.

792
00:36:09,010 --> 00:36:11,810
Do you think 100 samples M are going to be a good approximation

793
00:36:11,810 --> 00:36:13,830
to the integral over this 100-dimensional space?

794
00:36:13,830 --> 00:36:15,850
What do you think?

795
00:36:15,850 --> 00:36:17,910
Thumbs up if you think it will be OK?

796
00:36:17,910 --> 00:36:18,410
OK, no.

797
00:36:18,410 --> 00:36:19,790
Thumbs down if you think it won't be OK?

798
00:36:19,790 --> 00:36:20,670
Yeah, thumbs down.

799
00:36:20,670 --> 00:36:22,110
OK, so why?

800
00:36:22,110 --> 00:36:24,090
How many samples, roughly, would you

801
00:36:24,090 --> 00:36:28,010
say you need to have a good approximation to an integral

802
00:36:28,010 --> 00:36:32,450
over a D-dimensional space?

803
00:36:32,450 --> 00:36:36,050
This kind of gets at your question, I think.

804
00:36:36,050 --> 00:36:38,530
Yeah, in the back.

805
00:36:38,530 --> 00:36:40,990
AUDIENCE: Responding to his question, your question,

806
00:36:40,990 --> 00:36:45,130
the Monte Carlo estimator has variance 1 over M,

807
00:36:45,130 --> 00:36:46,850
so it goes down to square root--

808
00:36:46,850 --> 00:36:48,070
1 over square root of M.

809
00:36:48,070 --> 00:36:48,450
PHILLIP ISOLA: Excellent.

810
00:36:48,450 --> 00:36:49,150
OK, thank you.

811
00:36:49,150 --> 00:36:51,250
Monte Carlo estimator has variance 1 over M.

812
00:36:51,250 --> 00:36:52,750
AUDIENCE: Nominally non-dimensional,

813
00:36:52,750 --> 00:36:55,202
but in practice, somewhat dimensional.

814
00:36:55,202 --> 00:36:56,910
PHILLIP ISOLA: Nominally not dimensional,

815
00:36:56,910 --> 00:36:58,370
so actually, there's no dependence

816
00:36:58,370 --> 00:37:00,930
on the dimensionality D, I guess, but in practice, there

817
00:37:00,930 --> 00:37:01,570
is.

818
00:37:01,570 --> 00:37:03,070
OK.

819
00:37:03,070 --> 00:37:06,850
So I'll say that my rough intuition would be that you

820
00:37:06,850 --> 00:37:08,310
need exponential samples.

821
00:37:08,310 --> 00:37:13,170
In order to cover a D-dimensional space--

822
00:37:13,170 --> 00:37:15,090
and each dimension is going to be--

823
00:37:15,090 --> 00:37:16,507
you're going to have to fill it up

824
00:37:16,507 --> 00:37:19,390
with another fixed set of samples,

825
00:37:19,390 --> 00:37:21,730
so you should have something exponentially more samples

826
00:37:21,730 --> 00:37:23,530
to cover a D-dimensional space.

827
00:37:23,530 --> 00:37:26,857
But yeah, formally I'm not sure what--

828
00:37:26,857 --> 00:37:28,190
so actually, how does that work?

829
00:37:28,190 --> 00:37:30,410
I'm interested.

830
00:37:30,410 --> 00:37:31,170
Yeah.

831
00:37:31,170 --> 00:37:33,010
Central limit theorem?

832
00:37:33,010 --> 00:37:33,730
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

833
00:37:33,730 --> 00:37:34,657
The classical result.

834
00:37:34,657 --> 00:37:35,490
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

835
00:37:38,170 --> 00:37:40,370
OK.

836
00:37:40,370 --> 00:37:43,082
OK, well--

837
00:37:43,082 --> 00:37:44,050
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

838
00:37:44,050 --> 00:37:46,010
AUDIENCE: --multiple dimensions [INAUDIBLE]

839
00:37:46,010 --> 00:37:47,510
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [? it can happen. ?]

840
00:37:47,510 --> 00:37:49,530
We end up with a multi-dimensional Gaussian

841
00:37:49,530 --> 00:37:54,100
as your limit, and that has a multi-dimensional variance.

842
00:37:54,100 --> 00:37:56,780
And then if you want to calculate the error of that

843
00:37:56,780 --> 00:38:00,320
instead of variance, I think exponential, as I mentioned,

844
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:02,540
is exactly correct.

845
00:38:02,540 --> 00:38:05,060
PHILLIP ISOLA: I think it's exponential in dimension--

846
00:38:05,060 --> 00:38:07,620
so there should be some term in terms of--

847
00:38:07,620 --> 00:38:10,780
the number of samples you need to get some quality of fit

848
00:38:10,780 --> 00:38:12,360
should depend on the dimension.

849
00:38:12,360 --> 00:38:13,640
I think it's exponential in dimension,

850
00:38:13,640 --> 00:38:15,182
but there's probably more to discuss,

851
00:38:15,182 --> 00:38:16,700
maybe we can take that offline.

852
00:38:16,700 --> 00:38:19,060
But I'll just say that in practice, this

853
00:38:19,060 --> 00:38:22,020
doesn't work in high dimensions, and I

854
00:38:22,020 --> 00:38:24,375
think it's because you need exponentially more samples

855
00:38:24,375 --> 00:38:27,000
to get a good approximation to the integral in high dimensions,

856
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:29,042
but we should talk about that more offline to see

857
00:38:29,042 --> 00:38:30,580
if that's really true.

858
00:38:30,580 --> 00:38:31,420
OK.

859
00:38:31,420 --> 00:38:34,620
So, the next trick of the VAE is we're not

860
00:38:34,620 --> 00:38:36,700
going to just sample randomly, we're

861
00:38:36,700 --> 00:38:37,960
going to sample intelligently.

862
00:38:37,960 --> 00:38:40,703
We're not going to just do naive Monte Carlo approximation

863
00:38:40,703 --> 00:38:42,620
to the integral, we're going to do a much more

864
00:38:42,620 --> 00:38:44,820
intelligent approximation to the integral,

865
00:38:44,820 --> 00:38:48,000
and that one is going to be called importance sampling.

866
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:48,500
OK.

867
00:38:48,500 --> 00:38:50,600
So what is the idea of importance sampling?

868
00:38:50,600 --> 00:38:52,380
The idea is that let's say that I

869
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:55,580
want to estimate the likelihood that my model places

870
00:38:55,580 --> 00:38:59,100
on some data point x, that little x up there.

871
00:38:59,100 --> 00:39:02,460
OK, what I'm going to do with importance sampling is say,

872
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:05,580
I could say that that's the expectation of px given

873
00:39:05,580 --> 00:39:07,700
z with respect--

874
00:39:07,700 --> 00:39:10,580
taking expectation over z.

875
00:39:10,580 --> 00:39:13,220
And I could randomly sample the z's to calculate that

876
00:39:13,220 --> 00:39:15,540
expectation.

877
00:39:15,540 --> 00:39:18,100
But what will happen if I randomly sample the z's?

878
00:39:18,100 --> 00:39:21,580
Then almost every single px given

879
00:39:21,580 --> 00:39:25,180
z for most of the z's will place nearly zero probability

880
00:39:25,180 --> 00:39:27,100
on that data point x.

881
00:39:27,100 --> 00:39:31,760
Most of these mixture components don't lie near the data point x.

882
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:33,260
Just in practice, this is what might

883
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:35,593
happen if I've spread out these mixture components to be

884
00:39:35,593 --> 00:39:38,220
a good fit to the data density.

885
00:39:38,220 --> 00:39:40,920
OK, so the observation is that with random sampling,

886
00:39:40,920 --> 00:39:42,860
maybe I have to take around 7 samples

887
00:39:42,860 --> 00:39:45,540
to get one Gaussian component that's near x,

888
00:39:45,540 --> 00:39:48,180
and almost all the other components are adding 0--

889
00:39:48,180 --> 00:39:50,020
roughly 0, so it's like this.

890
00:39:50,020 --> 00:39:54,720
p of x is equal to the average of px given one sample plus px

891
00:39:54,720 --> 00:39:57,280
given the next sample plus px given the next sample,

892
00:39:57,280 --> 00:40:00,100
but most of these px given a sample are 0.

893
00:40:00,100 --> 00:40:02,920
So almost all the terms are nearly 0 except for the sample

894
00:40:02,920 --> 00:40:03,420
that--

895
00:40:03,420 --> 00:40:07,140
except for the z's that map to Gaussians that place

896
00:40:07,140 --> 00:40:10,540
high density over this x.

897
00:40:10,540 --> 00:40:13,180
So in this expectation, most of the terms

898
00:40:13,180 --> 00:40:17,620
are going to be 0 for any given point x.

899
00:40:17,620 --> 00:40:19,860
So we can exploit that by just trying to say,

900
00:40:19,860 --> 00:40:22,260
let me only take the sample z2, and that

901
00:40:22,260 --> 00:40:26,100
will be a good approximation to this expectation.

902
00:40:26,100 --> 00:40:27,500
And this is called--

903
00:40:27,500 --> 00:40:29,660
this is the idea behind importance sampling.

904
00:40:29,660 --> 00:40:33,340
So in order to approximate this expectation, which

905
00:40:33,340 --> 00:40:38,260
is that integral, I can simply multiply by q--

906
00:40:38,260 --> 00:40:43,840
by another density q, and I can multiply by qz divided by qz--

907
00:40:43,840 --> 00:40:47,860
so qz divided by qz will be 1 as long as qz is non-zero

908
00:40:47,860 --> 00:40:50,120
everywhere, which is just a technical condition,

909
00:40:50,120 --> 00:40:52,740
we need to make sure it has support everywhere.

910
00:40:52,740 --> 00:40:55,460
OK, so then I can--

911
00:40:55,460 --> 00:40:56,180
that's valid.

912
00:40:56,180 --> 00:40:57,958
So I'm just multiplying by 1.

913
00:40:57,958 --> 00:40:59,500
But the interesting thing here is now

914
00:40:59,500 --> 00:41:01,620
this appears as an expectation with respect

915
00:41:01,620 --> 00:41:05,820
to z samples from qz as opposed to z samples from pz.

916
00:41:05,820 --> 00:41:09,700
And if qz is a distribution which

917
00:41:09,700 --> 00:41:16,460
has placed high density only on the z's that actually contribute

918
00:41:16,460 --> 00:41:19,780
to the sum that actually have high probability of the data

919
00:41:19,780 --> 00:41:23,100
under that z, high px given z, then this

920
00:41:23,100 --> 00:41:26,860
will be an expectation which requires fewer samples

921
00:41:26,860 --> 00:41:33,180
to get a good estimate of the true integral.

922
00:41:33,180 --> 00:41:36,120
OK, so this is called importance sampling.

923
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:38,820
I'm sampling from a distribution that gives more importance

924
00:41:38,820 --> 00:41:43,220
to the points that matter.

925
00:41:43,220 --> 00:41:45,500
OK, so I won't prove this, but I'll just

926
00:41:45,500 --> 00:41:50,980
assert that the optimal distribution

927
00:41:50,980 --> 00:41:53,400
to sample from for performing this estimation.

928
00:41:53,400 --> 00:41:56,580
In a certain sense, I think the distribution that

929
00:41:56,580 --> 00:42:01,460
minimizes the variance over my estimator of this expectation,

930
00:42:01,460 --> 00:42:09,260
is to sample z's from exactly the density p Z given x.

931
00:42:09,260 --> 00:42:10,393
OK.

932
00:42:10,393 --> 00:42:11,560
So what does that look like?

933
00:42:11,560 --> 00:42:18,600
So it's like if I knew p Z given x, it's like, here's x.

934
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:20,980
So given x, these are the Z's, let's say, that

935
00:42:20,980 --> 00:42:22,778
place high probability on x.

936
00:42:22,778 --> 00:42:25,320
It's a distribution-- it might be a complicated distribution,

937
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:27,420
but most of the density-- like, this

938
00:42:27,420 --> 00:42:32,660
is the high-density region of p Z given x, is in a small region.

939
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:34,640
And if I could simply sample from those points,

940
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:38,260
it would suffice to get a good estimate of what

941
00:42:38,260 --> 00:42:40,258
would happen if I had sampled from everywhere

942
00:42:40,258 --> 00:42:42,300
because most other points will place zero density

943
00:42:42,300 --> 00:42:44,860
and they just won't contribute to that integral--

944
00:42:44,860 --> 00:42:47,380
or roughly zero density.

945
00:42:47,380 --> 00:42:49,580
OK.

946
00:42:49,580 --> 00:42:52,580
OK, so importance sampling is like now I just sample once

947
00:42:52,580 --> 00:42:55,540
from this distribution and I get as good an estimate

948
00:42:55,540 --> 00:42:58,440
as having sampled seven times with random sampling.

949
00:43:01,180 --> 00:43:03,360
OK, so here's the entire transformation.

950
00:43:03,360 --> 00:43:06,780
I'm going to estimate the likelihood that my model places

951
00:43:06,780 --> 00:43:09,340
on the data via this expectation,

952
00:43:09,340 --> 00:43:12,780
but I'm going to switch it to this other expectation

953
00:43:12,780 --> 00:43:16,760
where-- these two expectations are exactly equal to each other.

954
00:43:16,760 --> 00:43:20,300
That's because these two integrals

955
00:43:20,300 --> 00:43:23,340
are exactly equal to each other if q has

956
00:43:23,340 --> 00:43:26,780
non-zero support everywhere.

957
00:43:26,780 --> 00:43:31,100
But this one can be approximated with a Monte Carlo set of--

958
00:43:31,100 --> 00:43:33,220
a Monte Carlo approximation to this--

959
00:43:33,220 --> 00:43:36,420
expectation is going to be a better estimate, lower

960
00:43:36,420 --> 00:43:38,960
variance than this expectation.

961
00:43:41,700 --> 00:43:44,390
OK.

962
00:43:44,390 --> 00:43:47,330
So here's an importance sampling.

963
00:43:47,330 --> 00:43:51,550
Random sampling, I take random samples from z-- from pz.

964
00:43:51,550 --> 00:43:53,707
And most of them don't contribute to the sum.

965
00:43:53,707 --> 00:43:55,790
Importance sampling, I only sample from the points

966
00:43:55,790 --> 00:43:56,970
that contribute to the sum.

967
00:43:56,970 --> 00:44:00,350
I only sample from the points that the model

968
00:44:00,350 --> 00:44:04,190
thinks will place high probability on x.

969
00:44:04,190 --> 00:44:06,230
So the probability of z given x is the points

970
00:44:06,230 --> 00:44:08,670
that the model thinks are the z's that will

971
00:44:08,670 --> 00:44:12,130
place high probability on x.

972
00:44:12,130 --> 00:44:12,630
OK.

973
00:44:18,030 --> 00:44:18,730
Great.

974
00:44:18,730 --> 00:44:24,550
So, if I knew p Z given x, then this would be what I want to do.

975
00:44:24,550 --> 00:44:28,550
I just sample from p Z given x and I get a better approximation

976
00:44:28,550 --> 00:44:33,170
to that integral, and this will then work in high dimensions

977
00:44:33,170 --> 00:44:35,670
because for high dimensions, the volume is exponential in D,

978
00:44:35,670 --> 00:44:40,590
but the area of z-space that actually maps to any given point

979
00:44:40,590 --> 00:44:45,450
x might be very small under a particular model p.

980
00:44:45,450 --> 00:44:45,950
OK.

981
00:44:45,950 --> 00:44:48,950
But the problem is, I don't know p Z given

982
00:44:48,950 --> 00:44:51,050
x that might be another complicated distribution,

983
00:44:51,050 --> 00:44:52,550
and I now have to come up with a way

984
00:44:52,550 --> 00:44:55,190
of modeling that distribution.

985
00:44:55,190 --> 00:44:56,210
So here's how you do it.

986
00:44:56,210 --> 00:45:00,550
The next step in the VAE-- the third trick is you're

987
00:45:00,550 --> 00:45:04,602
going to try to model or predict the optimal sampling

988
00:45:04,602 --> 00:45:06,310
distribution, the sampling distribution p

989
00:45:06,310 --> 00:45:08,293
Z given x given x.

990
00:45:08,293 --> 00:45:09,710
You're going to train a model that

991
00:45:09,710 --> 00:45:13,910
will take x as input and output a density over Z,

992
00:45:13,910 --> 00:45:17,050
and that will be a model of p Z given x.

993
00:45:17,050 --> 00:45:18,550
OK, so now we get to where it starts

994
00:45:18,550 --> 00:45:19,530
to look like an autoencoder.

995
00:45:19,530 --> 00:45:22,110
It probably didn't look at all like an autoencoder until now,

996
00:45:22,110 --> 00:45:23,318
but what does that look like?

997
00:45:23,318 --> 00:45:24,510
That's an encoder.

998
00:45:24,510 --> 00:45:31,390
That's a mapping from x to a distribution over z-space.

999
00:45:31,390 --> 00:45:34,310
So our model of--

1000
00:45:34,310 --> 00:45:40,310
our model, which we'll call q, which is trying to approximate p

1001
00:45:40,310 --> 00:45:44,070
theta Z given x, so we're going to try to approximate that

1002
00:45:44,070 --> 00:45:47,470
with another prediction called q.

1003
00:45:47,470 --> 00:45:52,310
That density is going to be, just for simplicity, modeled

1004
00:45:52,310 --> 00:45:53,410
also as a Gaussian.

1005
00:45:53,410 --> 00:45:55,230
So we're going to take our x, we're

1006
00:45:55,230 --> 00:45:57,710
going to try to predict what part of z-space

1007
00:45:57,710 --> 00:46:01,710
would p place high probability on x.

1008
00:46:01,710 --> 00:46:06,910
So for what part of z-space is px given z high probability?

1009
00:46:06,910 --> 00:46:10,310
We're going to predict that by modeling

1010
00:46:10,310 --> 00:46:12,570
a distribution over z-space.

1011
00:46:12,570 --> 00:46:14,510
And we're going to make that a Gaussian.

1012
00:46:14,510 --> 00:46:17,050
It will be-- we'll take our neural network f,

1013
00:46:17,050 --> 00:46:19,830
it will output a mean and a variance, or maybe a covariance

1014
00:46:19,830 --> 00:46:24,750
if we wish, and that will be our model for pz given

1015
00:46:24,750 --> 00:46:26,410
x, and that model is called q.

1016
00:46:26,410 --> 00:46:28,110
And q has parameters psi, which is

1017
00:46:28,110 --> 00:46:33,130
a new set of weights and biases of this encoder function.

1018
00:46:33,130 --> 00:46:33,630
OK.

1019
00:46:36,790 --> 00:46:37,310
OK.

1020
00:46:37,310 --> 00:46:40,370
So, now we're going to get to the full picture.

1021
00:46:40,370 --> 00:46:42,010
So we have these two pieces.

1022
00:46:42,010 --> 00:46:45,310
We have the decoder function, which

1023
00:46:45,310 --> 00:46:47,933
is this probability model-- probability of x given z,

1024
00:46:47,933 --> 00:46:49,350
and we have this encoder function,

1025
00:46:49,350 --> 00:46:51,610
probability of z given x.

1026
00:46:51,610 --> 00:46:53,830
We're going to put them together now.

1027
00:46:53,830 --> 00:46:59,230
So how am I going to actually write down

1028
00:46:59,230 --> 00:47:02,230
the objective function for the encoder?

1029
00:47:02,230 --> 00:47:04,810
So the objective, we're going to say we want it--

1030
00:47:04,810 --> 00:47:10,950
we want the encoder to model a distribution pz given x,

1031
00:47:10,950 --> 00:47:12,830
and it's going to model that distribution

1032
00:47:12,830 --> 00:47:15,430
with another distribution parameterized

1033
00:47:15,430 --> 00:47:17,490
by mean and variance that defines a Gaussian,

1034
00:47:17,490 --> 00:47:18,940
and that's going to be qz given x.

1035
00:47:18,940 --> 00:47:21,190
And we're going to try to make those two distributions

1036
00:47:21,190 --> 00:47:21,650
the same.

1037
00:47:21,650 --> 00:47:23,567
So we're going to use the KL divergence to try

1038
00:47:23,567 --> 00:47:26,090
to minimize the difference between those two distributions.

1039
00:47:26,090 --> 00:47:29,830
Just a particular divergence measure of how different

1040
00:47:29,830 --> 00:47:32,710
our two distributions, we want to make them the same.

1041
00:47:32,710 --> 00:47:35,610
OK, so this is our objective for the problem of the encoder.

1042
00:47:35,610 --> 00:47:38,708
The problem of encoder is to minimize Jq.

1043
00:47:38,708 --> 00:47:40,750
Actually, I think I have a negative sign, so it's

1044
00:47:40,750 --> 00:47:42,950
going to be to maximize Jq.

1045
00:47:42,950 --> 00:47:47,510
So I'm just going to rewrite the KL divergence.

1046
00:47:47,510 --> 00:47:49,990
There's a little bit of algebra that you'll probably

1047
00:47:49,990 --> 00:47:52,870
want to look at in more detail on your own time,

1048
00:47:52,870 --> 00:47:56,590
but this is one way of writing out the definition of KL

1049
00:47:56,590 --> 00:47:57,510
divergence.

1050
00:47:57,510 --> 00:48:02,510
It's going to be, yeah, this equation.

1051
00:48:02,510 --> 00:48:06,910
It's going to be in-expectation sampling from the density

1052
00:48:06,910 --> 00:48:12,630
that q Z given x all of these terms.

1053
00:48:12,630 --> 00:48:14,170
OK.

1054
00:48:14,170 --> 00:48:21,470
So the learning problem for q is to maximize--

1055
00:48:21,470 --> 00:48:24,030
to minimize the KL divergence or maximize the negative KL

1056
00:48:24,030 --> 00:48:24,930
divergence.

1057
00:48:24,930 --> 00:48:27,190
So this is the learning problem for q.

1058
00:48:27,190 --> 00:48:30,590
It's trying to predict the distribution over latent

1059
00:48:30,590 --> 00:48:33,175
variables that will explain a given data point x.

1060
00:48:33,175 --> 00:48:34,550
We call that inference sometimes,

1061
00:48:34,550 --> 00:48:39,110
infer the latent variables that explain the data x.

1062
00:48:39,110 --> 00:48:40,630
I can take this equation and I can

1063
00:48:40,630 --> 00:48:44,350
notice that if I'm maximizing with respect to the parameters

1064
00:48:44,350 --> 00:48:46,890
of q, well, the parameters of q don't appear in this term,

1065
00:48:46,890 --> 00:48:48,590
so I'm going to just remove that term.

1066
00:48:48,590 --> 00:48:52,050
They don't affect the maximization problem,

1067
00:48:52,050 --> 00:48:54,910
they don't affect the arg max over the parameters psi,

1068
00:48:54,910 --> 00:48:57,070
the parameters of the encoder.

1069
00:48:57,070 --> 00:49:00,810
So now-- and we'll use this term a little bit later,

1070
00:49:00,810 --> 00:49:03,130
but this equation here will be J,

1071
00:49:03,130 --> 00:49:05,350
it will come back in a minute.

1072
00:49:05,350 --> 00:49:10,350
OK, so for our learning problem for q is to optimize this.

1073
00:49:10,350 --> 00:49:13,750
And this is an expectation, so I could optimize that again

1074
00:49:13,750 --> 00:49:18,910
by just taking a Monte Carlo estimate to the expectation

1075
00:49:18,910 --> 00:49:21,990
by sampling a bunch of different z's and taking

1076
00:49:21,990 --> 00:49:27,950
the average over all of the values within the expectation.

1077
00:49:27,950 --> 00:49:28,700
OK.

1078
00:49:32,684 --> 00:49:36,030
So that's the learning problem for q,

1079
00:49:36,030 --> 00:49:39,350
which is parameterized by a neural network f.

1080
00:49:39,350 --> 00:49:41,910
Map from x to an inferred distribution

1081
00:49:41,910 --> 00:49:46,050
over latent variables that can explain x.

1082
00:49:46,050 --> 00:49:47,993
OK, the learning problem for p-- this

1083
00:49:47,993 --> 00:49:49,410
was our original learning problem.

1084
00:49:49,410 --> 00:49:52,470
We wanted to learn a generative model parameterized

1085
00:49:52,470 --> 00:49:58,110
by a neural net g where g places Gaussian probability

1086
00:49:58,110 --> 00:50:03,030
over the data space x, and g defines an infinite mixture

1087
00:50:03,030 --> 00:50:05,190
of Gaussians by mapping from-- by taking

1088
00:50:05,190 --> 00:50:08,510
an integral over all possible points in z-space.

1089
00:50:08,510 --> 00:50:11,390
OK, so for a learning problem for p,

1090
00:50:11,390 --> 00:50:15,670
what I'm going to call Jp, is to find the parameters theta that

1091
00:50:15,670 --> 00:50:19,430
maximize the likelihood that my model places on the data,

1092
00:50:19,430 --> 00:50:21,950
and with importance sampling, that

1093
00:50:21,950 --> 00:50:24,750
means I'm going to sample from my importance sampling

1094
00:50:24,750 --> 00:50:28,470
distribution q, which we learned the optimal importance sampling

1095
00:50:28,470 --> 00:50:31,270
distribution q up here, we know that it should be pz given x,

1096
00:50:31,270 --> 00:50:33,240
but we're just going to try to learn z

1097
00:50:33,240 --> 00:50:36,760
given x by fitting a distribution q to pz given x.

1098
00:50:36,760 --> 00:50:38,760
And this is, then, my importance sampling

1099
00:50:38,760 --> 00:50:40,560
for estimating the integral that defines

1100
00:50:40,560 --> 00:50:43,160
the likelihood of the data under the parameters theta

1101
00:50:43,160 --> 00:50:45,840
of my model neural net g.

1102
00:50:45,840 --> 00:50:46,800
OK.

1103
00:50:46,800 --> 00:50:50,920
So I know there's a lot of terms in math going on.

1104
00:50:50,920 --> 00:50:54,120
This might take you time to work through step by step.

1105
00:50:54,120 --> 00:50:58,240
All of this derivation is in the reading for today,

1106
00:50:58,240 --> 00:51:01,238
so these slides follow that almost exactly.

1107
00:51:01,238 --> 00:51:02,780
And additionally, in the problem set,

1108
00:51:02,780 --> 00:51:04,822
you'll get to work through some of this yourself,

1109
00:51:04,822 --> 00:51:06,840
so hopefully you'll enjoy that.

1110
00:51:06,840 --> 00:51:10,040
But I know this is a little more mathematically notation-heavy

1111
00:51:10,040 --> 00:51:12,080
than some of the other lectures.

1112
00:51:12,080 --> 00:51:12,830
OK.

1113
00:51:12,830 --> 00:51:15,300
So now putting everything together,

1114
00:51:15,300 --> 00:51:18,400
there's one little final step, which

1115
00:51:18,400 --> 00:51:25,160
is, it turns out that if we approximate log of expectation

1116
00:51:25,160 --> 00:51:27,220
by taking sample like a Monte Carlo estimate--

1117
00:51:27,220 --> 00:51:31,680
by taking samples from the thing that we're taking

1118
00:51:31,680 --> 00:51:34,580
the expectation over, if we do a Monte Carlo estimate of this

1119
00:51:34,580 --> 00:51:36,372
and then we take log of that, it turns out,

1120
00:51:36,372 --> 00:51:39,245
that's a biased estimator of log of expectation.

1121
00:51:39,245 --> 00:51:41,120
So Monte Carlo estimate of log of expectation

1122
00:51:41,120 --> 00:51:42,820
is a biased estimator, and we don't really want that.

1123
00:51:42,820 --> 00:51:45,195
We want it to be the case that as samples go to infinity,

1124
00:51:45,195 --> 00:51:47,880
we get the true answer, and that won't be the case here.

1125
00:51:47,880 --> 00:51:49,755
So we're going to do this little trick, which

1126
00:51:49,755 --> 00:51:53,128
is that we're going to pull the log inside the expectation

1127
00:51:53,128 --> 00:51:55,420
so that then we just have expectation of some function,

1128
00:51:55,420 --> 00:51:57,800
and then a Monte Carlo estimate of that expectation

1129
00:51:57,800 --> 00:52:01,240
is an unbiased estimator of that expectation.

1130
00:52:01,240 --> 00:52:01,740
OK.

1131
00:52:01,740 --> 00:52:04,720
So we're allowed to do that by Jensen's inequality that

1132
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:07,760
says log of e of some function is

1133
00:52:07,760 --> 00:52:10,880
equal-- is greater than or equal to expectation

1134
00:52:10,880 --> 00:52:14,120
of log of that function.

1135
00:52:14,120 --> 00:52:16,060
So this is just an identity, again, from--

1136
00:52:16,060 --> 00:52:18,060
a lot of you will have seen Jensen's inequality.

1137
00:52:18,060 --> 00:52:20,440
It comes up a lot when you're working with probabilities.

1138
00:52:20,440 --> 00:52:23,160
And now what we have is that this

1139
00:52:23,160 --> 00:52:26,400
is going to be a lower bound to our objective J.

1140
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:27,900
So this is a lower bound.

1141
00:52:27,900 --> 00:52:29,820
I can rewrite it-- so I take the log,

1142
00:52:29,820 --> 00:52:31,660
I move around the terms a little bit.

1143
00:52:31,660 --> 00:52:34,440
This is just rewriting some algebra.

1144
00:52:34,440 --> 00:52:40,000
And this thing here is called the evidence lower bound.

1145
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:41,052
It's called the ELBO.

1146
00:52:41,052 --> 00:52:42,760
So you'll come across this term if you're

1147
00:52:42,760 --> 00:52:45,040
working in VAEs or other types of what

1148
00:52:45,040 --> 00:52:47,160
we call variational inference.

1149
00:52:47,160 --> 00:52:50,460
And it's a lower bound to the thing we actually care about,

1150
00:52:50,460 --> 00:52:53,398
which is the likelihood of the data under the model.

1151
00:52:53,398 --> 00:52:55,440
And it's a lower bound due to Jensen's inequality

1152
00:52:55,440 --> 00:52:56,880
on this line.

1153
00:52:56,880 --> 00:53:00,360
And this is something that is now--

1154
00:53:00,360 --> 00:53:02,520
we can approximate this expectation as an--

1155
00:53:02,520 --> 00:53:06,520
or this expectation with samples and it'll

1156
00:53:06,520 --> 00:53:08,420
be an unbiased estimator of that value.

1157
00:53:08,420 --> 00:53:10,795
So we're going to have an unbiased estimator of the lower

1158
00:53:10,795 --> 00:53:12,880
bound, we can maximize the lower bound

1159
00:53:12,880 --> 00:53:15,380
on the likelihood the model applies to the data,

1160
00:53:15,380 --> 00:53:17,040
and this will learn a model that places

1161
00:53:17,040 --> 00:53:18,760
high likelihood on the data.

1162
00:53:18,760 --> 00:53:26,040
Maximizing the lower bound will increase my objective J. OK.

1163
00:53:26,040 --> 00:53:29,000
So evidence-- like, this is the evidence

1164
00:53:29,000 --> 00:53:33,597
that the model provides for the data.

1165
00:53:33,597 --> 00:53:35,680
Anyway, yeah, don't worry about the name too much,

1166
00:53:35,680 --> 00:53:37,720
but it's called the evidence lower-bound,

1167
00:53:37,720 --> 00:53:39,360
it's just terminology, ELBO.

1168
00:53:39,360 --> 00:53:39,860
OK.

1169
00:53:39,860 --> 00:53:46,000
So it comes in two terms that both have intuitive form,

1170
00:53:46,000 --> 00:53:47,820
and I'll get to those on the next slide.

1171
00:53:47,820 --> 00:53:50,960
But the interesting thing is, once we have written things

1172
00:53:50,960 --> 00:53:54,440
in this form, what you can notice, if you go back a slide,

1173
00:53:54,440 --> 00:53:56,680
is that this objective is actually

1174
00:53:56,680 --> 00:54:02,220
exactly equal to the objective that we had for q.

1175
00:54:02,220 --> 00:54:06,340
So q appears over here in this KL term.

1176
00:54:06,340 --> 00:54:07,340
So what is this saying?

1177
00:54:07,340 --> 00:54:10,560
This is saying that in order to learn

1178
00:54:10,560 --> 00:54:15,060
the parameters of the encoder that give me the best importance

1179
00:54:15,060 --> 00:54:18,320
sampling distribution for estimating

1180
00:54:18,320 --> 00:54:22,720
the likelihood the decoder places on the data,

1181
00:54:22,720 --> 00:54:27,960
it actually works out that I can just

1182
00:54:27,960 --> 00:54:31,240
maximize this evidence lower bound

1183
00:54:31,240 --> 00:54:36,520
with respect to the encoder and decoder parameters jointly.

1184
00:54:36,520 --> 00:54:38,020
OK.

1185
00:54:38,020 --> 00:54:43,600
So, the VAE objective now can be written in this form

1186
00:54:43,600 --> 00:54:45,760
as maximizing this lower bound to the thing

1187
00:54:45,760 --> 00:54:48,280
we actually care about, and I can approximate that

1188
00:54:48,280 --> 00:54:52,200
with sampling, and it will be an unbiased estimator of all

1189
00:54:52,200 --> 00:54:54,600
of these expectations and integrals.

1190
00:54:54,600 --> 00:54:55,585
It has two terms.

1191
00:54:55,585 --> 00:54:57,960
It has the first term, which is how much probability does

1192
00:54:57,960 --> 00:55:00,400
the model place on the data?

1193
00:55:00,400 --> 00:55:02,040
And another term, which is, well, I

1194
00:55:02,040 --> 00:55:03,720
was estimating how much probability

1195
00:55:03,720 --> 00:55:06,400
the model places on the data with respect to the wrong--

1196
00:55:06,400 --> 00:55:08,420
not actually taking samples over all of z-space,

1197
00:55:08,420 --> 00:55:10,080
but with taking samples with respect

1198
00:55:10,080 --> 00:55:11,717
to this other distribution.

1199
00:55:11,717 --> 00:55:13,300
So I have to somehow account for that,

1200
00:55:13,300 --> 00:55:16,300
so I'll penalize the difference between this other distribution

1201
00:55:16,300 --> 00:55:18,380
in my prior, the unit Gaussian pz.

1202
00:55:21,280 --> 00:55:22,030
OK.

1203
00:55:22,030 --> 00:55:24,600
So this term's coming out because I

1204
00:55:24,600 --> 00:55:26,340
had to multiply by this ratio.

1205
00:55:26,340 --> 00:55:29,200
How much different are my samples

1206
00:55:29,200 --> 00:55:32,360
under pz from my samples under qz?

1207
00:55:32,360 --> 00:55:35,960
OK, so that pops out into this term here.

1208
00:55:35,960 --> 00:55:39,640
So now, the encoder is trying to make these things as

1209
00:55:39,640 --> 00:55:41,560
similar as possible, and the decoder

1210
00:55:41,560 --> 00:55:44,280
is trying to just learn parameters theta that

1211
00:55:44,280 --> 00:55:48,100
will maximize the probability placed on the data.

1212
00:55:50,960 --> 00:55:53,410
OK.

1213
00:55:53,410 --> 00:55:56,200
So here's the picture.

1214
00:55:56,200 --> 00:55:58,280
I'm going to start with a data point x.

1215
00:55:58,280 --> 00:55:59,880
I will encode it.

1216
00:55:59,880 --> 00:56:02,500
That will define a distribution over my latent space.

1217
00:56:02,500 --> 00:56:04,680
I'll take a sample from that distribution, that's

1218
00:56:04,680 --> 00:56:07,880
one important sample, for estimating the probability

1219
00:56:07,880 --> 00:56:09,305
of my model places on the data.

1220
00:56:09,305 --> 00:56:10,680
And then I'll decode that and see

1221
00:56:10,680 --> 00:56:13,800
what probability the model did place on the data.

1222
00:56:13,800 --> 00:56:16,320
That's one important sampling step

1223
00:56:16,320 --> 00:56:20,160
of approximating the lower bound to the probability

1224
00:56:20,160 --> 00:56:23,640
that my model places on the data.

1225
00:56:23,640 --> 00:56:25,040
OK.

1226
00:56:25,040 --> 00:56:27,520
So, now we come back to autoencoder.

1227
00:56:27,520 --> 00:56:30,300
A lot of math, but at the end of the day,

1228
00:56:30,300 --> 00:56:31,880
the VAE is a very simple model that

1229
00:56:31,880 --> 00:56:33,288
looks just like an autoencoder.

1230
00:56:33,288 --> 00:56:35,080
So here's how it looks like an autoencoder.

1231
00:56:35,080 --> 00:56:39,520
We start with data point x, we encode it, we get mean mu z

1232
00:56:39,520 --> 00:56:42,720
and variance sigma squared z.

1233
00:56:42,720 --> 00:56:47,940
We then take a sample from the distribution q Z given x.

1234
00:56:47,940 --> 00:56:51,260
So taking a sample, that means we're just going to add noise.

1235
00:56:51,260 --> 00:56:54,880
So remember that mu z plus unit Gaussian epsilon

1236
00:56:54,880 --> 00:56:59,160
times standard deviation for the encoded

1237
00:56:59,160 --> 00:57:02,520
point x-- so sigma z is going to be the standard deviation

1238
00:57:02,520 --> 00:57:05,200
of the encoded point x.

1239
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:08,120
This is one sample from a normal distribution centered

1240
00:57:08,120 --> 00:57:11,160
at mu z with variance sigma z squared.

1241
00:57:11,160 --> 00:57:13,680
OK, I think I'm getting the squared versus not squared term

1242
00:57:13,680 --> 00:57:15,640
correct now.

1243
00:57:15,640 --> 00:57:20,430
So, that is one sample, for importance sampling,

1244
00:57:20,430 --> 00:57:26,010
for estimating the density that my model will place on the data.

1245
00:57:26,010 --> 00:57:28,770
So then I will decode that to observe,

1246
00:57:28,770 --> 00:57:30,250
under a normal distribution, what

1247
00:57:30,250 --> 00:57:32,490
is the probability that my model placed on that data

1248
00:57:32,490 --> 00:57:33,865
for that mixture component, which

1249
00:57:33,865 --> 00:57:36,570
is a good approximation to the entire integral.

1250
00:57:36,570 --> 00:57:38,650
OK, so remember the normal distribution is just

1251
00:57:38,650 --> 00:57:43,170
an analytical form, so I can calculate how much probability

1252
00:57:43,170 --> 00:57:47,410
g placed on the data point x.

1253
00:57:47,410 --> 00:57:49,250
OK, so this looks just like an autoencoder

1254
00:57:49,250 --> 00:57:52,890
except one step, which is this noise I added in the latent

1255
00:57:52,890 --> 00:57:54,670
space.

1256
00:57:54,670 --> 00:57:56,190
Otherwise, it's just encode-decode,

1257
00:57:56,190 --> 00:57:59,170
but I added a little bit of noise to the latent space

1258
00:57:59,170 --> 00:58:00,430
because that's my sampling.

1259
00:58:00,430 --> 00:58:02,530
That's necessary because I'm sampling

1260
00:58:02,530 --> 00:58:05,530
to approximate this expectation.

1261
00:58:05,530 --> 00:58:07,410
OK, so here's the two terms of the ELBO

1262
00:58:07,410 --> 00:58:09,890
that I talked about before.

1263
00:58:09,890 --> 00:58:14,510
The first term, it looks like a reconstruction error.

1264
00:58:14,510 --> 00:58:15,270
Why?

1265
00:58:15,270 --> 00:58:18,730
Because p theta x given z is going

1266
00:58:18,730 --> 00:58:27,770
to be a normal distribution over x centered at a mean, which

1267
00:58:27,770 --> 00:58:32,190
is the decoded g theta of z.

1268
00:58:32,190 --> 00:58:35,230
So a normal distribution over x is just going to be-- a log

1269
00:58:35,230 --> 00:58:37,510
normal distribution will be just the squared error.

1270
00:58:37,510 --> 00:58:40,330
If I take log of e to the negative squared distance,

1271
00:58:40,330 --> 00:58:43,050
it's just negative squared distance.

1272
00:58:43,050 --> 00:58:49,010
So how different is my decoded point from my input observation

1273
00:58:49,010 --> 00:58:50,450
x?

1274
00:58:50,450 --> 00:58:53,850
That's this likelihood term.

1275
00:58:53,850 --> 00:58:56,348
And these other terms over here are just--

1276
00:58:56,348 --> 00:58:57,890
because the Gaussian distribution has

1277
00:58:57,890 --> 00:58:59,890
to be normalized, and the variance

1278
00:58:59,890 --> 00:59:02,770
appears in the normalization, and dividing by 2 sigma squared

1279
00:59:02,770 --> 00:59:04,970
is just also part of the definition of the Gaussian

1280
00:59:04,970 --> 00:59:07,730
probability.

1281
00:59:07,730 --> 00:59:12,450
So just for simplicity, imagine that we're going to model--

1282
00:59:12,450 --> 00:59:14,850
we're going to use a VAE where we won't actually

1283
00:59:14,850 --> 00:59:17,977
model the covariance or even the variance of the Gaussians.

1284
00:59:17,977 --> 00:59:19,310
This is a valid modeling choice.

1285
00:59:19,310 --> 00:59:23,635
So I could just fix my circles to have radius 1.

1286
00:59:23,635 --> 00:59:25,010
I can say I'm going to try to fit

1287
00:59:25,010 --> 00:59:26,730
an infinite mixture of Gaussians where

1288
00:59:26,730 --> 00:59:29,070
I don't get to change the variance of the circles,

1289
00:59:29,070 --> 00:59:31,487
but I'm still going to try to find the max likelihood fit.

1290
00:59:31,487 --> 00:59:33,450
That's a perfectly valid modeling choice.

1291
00:59:33,450 --> 00:59:36,110
And if I did that, then sigma becomes 1,

1292
00:59:36,110 --> 00:59:38,570
and these terms all become constant.

1293
00:59:38,570 --> 00:59:41,650
And then you can see that really, all I would be doing

1294
00:59:41,650 --> 00:59:46,130
is trying to minimize the difference between my data point

1295
00:59:46,130 --> 00:59:49,170
x and encode-decode of the data point x.

1296
00:59:49,170 --> 00:59:52,010
So then it would be exactly the autoencoder objective

1297
00:59:52,010 --> 00:59:54,930
of minimizing the mean squared error between my reconstructions

1298
00:59:54,930 --> 00:59:58,010
and my input observation.

1299
00:59:58,010 --> 01:00:02,150
So VAEs often will also model the variance of this Gaussian,

1300
01:00:02,150 --> 01:00:03,890
but vanilla autoencoders don't really

1301
01:00:03,890 --> 01:00:05,348
model the variance of the Gaussian,

1302
01:00:05,348 --> 01:00:08,890
they just kind of implicitly set it to be constant.

1303
01:00:08,890 --> 01:00:11,050
OK.

1304
01:00:11,050 --> 01:00:14,470
OK, the other term, the KL, is saying, well,

1305
01:00:14,470 --> 01:00:19,430
I need my q Z given x distribution.

1306
01:00:19,430 --> 01:00:21,830
I want it to be close to a unit Gaussian.

1307
01:00:21,830 --> 01:00:26,650
So we say that the posterior of the inference direction

1308
01:00:26,650 --> 01:00:31,390
should be near the prior pz So what is this?

1309
01:00:31,390 --> 01:00:33,702
This term also has a very simple interpretation.

1310
01:00:33,702 --> 01:00:34,910
So here's what it looks like.

1311
01:00:34,910 --> 01:00:38,530
This is the KL-- this is the KL divergence between two

1312
01:00:38,530 --> 01:00:39,470
Gaussians.

1313
01:00:39,470 --> 01:00:41,970
So luckily, the KL divergence between two Gaussians

1314
01:00:41,970 --> 01:00:45,710
has a closed form, an analytical form, and it looks like that.

1315
01:00:45,710 --> 01:00:50,210
So remember, this is the KL divergence between the Gaussian

1316
01:00:50,210 --> 01:00:55,490
defined by q Z given x and the prior Gaussian, which

1317
01:00:55,490 --> 01:00:56,750
is a unit Gaussian.

1318
01:00:56,750 --> 01:00:59,010
So this is the KL divergence with respect to--

1319
01:00:59,010 --> 01:01:03,230
between a Gaussian that has mean mu and variance sigma squared,

1320
01:01:03,230 --> 01:01:06,930
and a unit Gaussian that has mean 0 and variance 1.

1321
01:01:06,930 --> 01:01:07,430
OK.

1322
01:01:07,430 --> 01:01:09,050
It has this form.

1323
01:01:09,050 --> 01:01:11,710
In the readings, we posted the original VAE paper,

1324
01:01:11,710 --> 01:01:14,890
and in the appendix, there's a nice derivation of that form.

1325
01:01:14,890 --> 01:01:16,223
OK, so what does this look like?

1326
01:01:16,223 --> 01:01:17,973
Well, let's, again, imagine that we're not

1327
01:01:17,973 --> 01:01:19,410
going to model the variance, we're

1328
01:01:19,410 --> 01:01:22,910
just going to set the variance to be one constant.

1329
01:01:22,910 --> 01:01:27,490
We're only going to get to update our neural net to improve

1330
01:01:27,490 --> 01:01:29,130
the density by changing the means

1331
01:01:29,130 --> 01:01:31,410
that it places over the data.

1332
01:01:31,410 --> 01:01:34,470
OK, so these terms would then can be ignored, they go away,

1333
01:01:34,470 --> 01:01:35,503
this is just a constant.

1334
01:01:35,503 --> 01:01:36,170
So what is that?

1335
01:01:36,170 --> 01:01:43,270
That's going to be I want to minimize the means.

1336
01:01:43,270 --> 01:01:45,610
So basically, if all these terms are constant,

1337
01:01:45,610 --> 01:01:47,930
one half is just a scaling, in order

1338
01:01:47,930 --> 01:01:52,390
to increase the evidence lower bound,

1339
01:01:52,390 --> 01:01:54,630
in order to place higher probability on the data,

1340
01:01:54,630 --> 01:01:57,255
increase the lower bound to the likelihood I place on the data,

1341
01:01:57,255 --> 01:02:00,850
I'm going to try to decrease the means.

1342
01:02:00,850 --> 01:02:02,810
So all of this term is saying is squash

1343
01:02:02,810 --> 01:02:06,410
the means toward the origin.

1344
01:02:06,410 --> 01:02:09,112
So I'm going to try to reconstruct my data-- that's

1345
01:02:09,112 --> 01:02:11,570
the first term, that's the data reconstruction term, that's

1346
01:02:11,570 --> 01:02:12,550
an autoencoder.

1347
01:02:12,550 --> 01:02:15,530
And I have one extra term, which is just do that in a way

1348
01:02:15,530 --> 01:02:19,450
where all of the encodings are near the origin.

1349
01:02:19,450 --> 01:02:20,630
So what does that look like?

1350
01:02:20,630 --> 01:02:22,710
That means that this funny shape that I could have gotten,

1351
01:02:22,710 --> 01:02:23,710
this is an autoencoder--

1352
01:02:23,710 --> 01:02:26,450
I just encode, I decode, I minimize reconstruction,

1353
01:02:26,450 --> 01:02:29,350
I might get an arbitrarily complicated latent space.

1354
01:02:29,350 --> 01:02:32,410
In fact, here, if my latent space is two-dimensional

1355
01:02:32,410 --> 01:02:35,125
on this cartoon, I could just have f be the identity,

1356
01:02:35,125 --> 01:02:37,250
and then my latent space has exactly the same shape

1357
01:02:37,250 --> 01:02:39,770
as my input data space.

1358
01:02:39,770 --> 01:02:41,250
If g and f are both the identity,

1359
01:02:41,250 --> 01:02:43,790
it's just, like, I encode to the same shape,

1360
01:02:43,790 --> 01:02:46,170
I decode back to the same shape.

1361
01:02:46,170 --> 01:02:49,270
That would satisfy the reconstruction error,

1362
01:02:49,270 --> 01:02:53,190
but it wouldn't be a very good representation of the data,

1363
01:02:53,190 --> 01:02:56,330
it would just be the raw data itself once again.

1364
01:02:56,330 --> 01:02:58,970
So the variational autoencoder simply

1365
01:02:58,970 --> 01:03:00,730
says, no, you've got to squish that latent

1366
01:03:00,730 --> 01:03:02,310
space toward the origin.

1367
01:03:06,230 --> 01:03:08,963
So this means that in a normal autoencoder,

1368
01:03:08,963 --> 01:03:11,130
I need to make sure the dimensionality of the latent

1369
01:03:11,130 --> 01:03:13,450
space is low so that I'm doing compression and forcing

1370
01:03:13,450 --> 01:03:14,990
it to do something non-trivial.

1371
01:03:14,990 --> 01:03:18,155
In a variational autoencoder, it helps to have a low-dimensional

1372
01:03:18,155 --> 01:03:20,530
latent space, but it's less critical because there's this

1373
01:03:20,530 --> 01:03:24,090
other term, this KL term, which is trying to compress

1374
01:03:24,090 --> 01:03:27,290
the distribution toward 0--

1375
01:03:27,290 --> 01:03:29,650
compress all the means towards 0.

1376
01:03:29,650 --> 01:03:32,010
And equivalently, we can understand

1377
01:03:32,010 --> 01:03:37,010
that to be, I'm trying to make the distribution of encodings z

1378
01:03:37,010 --> 01:03:38,560
have the shape of a unit Gaussian,

1379
01:03:38,560 --> 01:03:40,810
but when I'm not modeling the variance that just means

1380
01:03:40,810 --> 01:03:43,410
squishing the means towards 0.

1381
01:03:43,410 --> 01:03:44,290
OK.

1382
01:03:44,290 --> 01:03:45,990
So I just squish everything down.

1383
01:03:45,990 --> 01:03:47,830
Why does it not collapse to a single point?

1384
01:03:47,830 --> 01:03:48,930
So what do you think?

1385
01:03:48,930 --> 01:03:51,927
If I'm trying to squish the means towards 0,

1386
01:03:51,927 --> 01:03:53,510
why don't they just all collapse to 0?

1387
01:03:53,510 --> 01:03:55,570
What do you think?

1388
01:03:55,570 --> 01:03:56,390
What prevents that?

1389
01:03:56,390 --> 01:03:56,890
Yeah?

1390
01:04:00,050 --> 01:04:01,190
I think I heard an answer.

1391
01:04:01,190 --> 01:04:01,690
No?

1392
01:04:01,690 --> 01:04:03,290
AUDIENCE: Would it push everything to 0?

1393
01:04:03,290 --> 01:04:04,630
PHILLIP ISOLA: If you push everything to 0--

1394
01:04:04,630 --> 01:04:05,530
AUDIENCE: That's trivial.

1395
01:04:05,530 --> 01:04:06,270
PHILLIP ISOLA: It's trivial.

1396
01:04:06,270 --> 01:04:08,480
And what will happen to the reconstruction?

1397
01:04:08,480 --> 01:04:09,520
AUDIENCE: You cannot.

1398
01:04:09,520 --> 01:04:10,120
PHILLIP ISOLA: You cannot.

1399
01:04:10,120 --> 01:04:10,620
Yeah.

1400
01:04:10,620 --> 01:04:11,660
So that's the tension.

1401
01:04:11,660 --> 01:04:13,220
We're going to try to push everything

1402
01:04:13,220 --> 01:04:17,340
to 0, that's this term if I'm not modeling the variance.

1403
01:04:17,340 --> 01:04:20,380
But I also need to satisfy this term, which says that you better

1404
01:04:20,380 --> 01:04:22,580
be able to reconstruct your input point,

1405
01:04:22,580 --> 01:04:25,078
and if everything goes to 0, then there's ambiguity.

1406
01:04:25,078 --> 01:04:26,620
So in order to reconstruct the input,

1407
01:04:26,620 --> 01:04:28,515
I need to to preserve information.

1408
01:04:28,515 --> 01:04:29,640
I can't compress too much--

1409
01:04:29,640 --> 01:04:31,483
I can't squish everything down too much,

1410
01:04:31,483 --> 01:04:32,900
so you want to spread out in order

1411
01:04:32,900 --> 01:04:34,763
to be able to reconstruct the data.

1412
01:04:34,763 --> 01:04:36,180
And that's the fundamental tension

1413
01:04:36,180 --> 01:04:37,700
of the variational autoencoder.

1414
01:04:37,700 --> 01:04:40,680
Pushes everything toward the 0, creates this compact shape.

1415
01:04:40,680 --> 01:04:43,190
It's like I have some Play-Doh, and I'm

1416
01:04:43,190 --> 01:04:44,940
trying to squish it down, but there's also

1417
01:04:44,940 --> 01:04:46,690
this physical force which is preventing it

1418
01:04:46,690 --> 01:04:48,140
from going to a black hole.

1419
01:04:48,140 --> 01:04:50,940
It's like, no, no, I don't want to do that because I

1420
01:04:50,940 --> 01:04:53,220
need to still be able to know where I came from

1421
01:04:53,220 --> 01:04:54,580
to reconstruct the data.

1422
01:04:54,580 --> 01:04:55,320
It's like that.

1423
01:04:55,320 --> 01:04:57,278
I'm pressing it down, but there's some tension.

1424
01:04:57,278 --> 01:04:59,180
Eventually it gets to this unit shape,

1425
01:04:59,180 --> 01:05:01,480
and that's what the math works out to.

1426
01:05:01,480 --> 01:05:02,980
So here's what that looks like.

1427
01:05:02,980 --> 01:05:05,620
So now here, I'm showing the encoder mapping

1428
01:05:05,620 --> 01:05:06,680
at initialization.

1429
01:05:06,680 --> 01:05:09,780
The neural net is initialized with weights and biases that

1430
01:05:09,780 --> 01:05:12,380
are near 0, so everything maps to 0.

1431
01:05:12,380 --> 01:05:13,480
And then the decoder.

1432
01:05:13,480 --> 01:05:17,340
So initially, all of my mixture components are near 0.

1433
01:05:17,340 --> 01:05:19,340
And here's what it looks like to train.

1434
01:05:19,340 --> 01:05:22,940
So what you'll notice is I'm giving--

1435
01:05:22,940 --> 01:05:26,693
I'm getting a good coverage over the data distribution.

1436
01:05:26,693 --> 01:05:28,360
That's my infinite mixture of Gaussians,

1437
01:05:28,360 --> 01:05:30,027
but I'm visualizing it with a finite set

1438
01:05:30,027 --> 01:05:31,820
of samples along this grid.

1439
01:05:31,820 --> 01:05:33,740
And what is the encoder doing?

1440
01:05:33,740 --> 01:05:38,260
It's like, I want to spread out these Gaussians so

1441
01:05:38,260 --> 01:05:42,020
that if I go from x to z and reconstruct,

1442
01:05:42,020 --> 01:05:44,620
I'll know where I came from, I'll have enough information,

1443
01:05:44,620 --> 01:05:49,040
but I also want to be near the unit sphere, unit Gaussian.

1444
01:05:49,040 --> 01:05:51,340
So this tension will make it pop out

1445
01:05:51,340 --> 01:05:53,238
and then squish back together.

1446
01:05:53,238 --> 01:05:54,780
And you can think of this almost like

1447
01:05:54,780 --> 01:05:55,960
some sphere-packing problem.

1448
01:05:55,960 --> 01:05:57,793
There's probably some interesting connection

1449
01:05:57,793 --> 01:06:00,200
to a geometric way of understanding this,

1450
01:06:00,200 --> 01:06:02,180
that if you have a bunch of spheres

1451
01:06:02,180 --> 01:06:03,940
and you want to fit them all into--

1452
01:06:03,940 --> 01:06:05,960
you want to make them not overlap.

1453
01:06:05,960 --> 01:06:07,680
If they overlap, what's the problem?

1454
01:06:07,680 --> 01:06:08,980
That means there's ambiguity.

1455
01:06:08,980 --> 01:06:11,860
If two different data points map to the same point,

1456
01:06:11,860 --> 01:06:14,280
or overlapping Gaussians, then there's ambiguity,

1457
01:06:14,280 --> 01:06:16,560
you don't know which one it came from, and you can't reconstruct.

1458
01:06:16,560 --> 01:06:18,280
So it's like, you don't want them to overlap too much,

1459
01:06:18,280 --> 01:06:20,322
but you want them to squish down at the same time

1460
01:06:20,322 --> 01:06:21,460
and there's this tension.

1461
01:06:21,460 --> 01:06:23,002
And then the solution is that they'll

1462
01:06:23,002 --> 01:06:26,480
spread out to evenly tile the unit Gaussian.

1463
01:06:31,020 --> 01:06:31,780
OK.

1464
01:06:31,780 --> 01:06:33,620
So here's what the densities look like if I plot them

1465
01:06:33,620 --> 01:06:34,280
as a heat map.

1466
01:06:34,280 --> 01:06:35,960
So remember, these are the Gaussian components,

1467
01:06:35,960 --> 01:06:37,335
but actually, they're continuous.

1468
01:06:37,335 --> 01:06:39,240
They're little blips of Gaussian probability.

1469
01:06:39,240 --> 01:06:41,032
So here's what it looks like as a heat map,

1470
01:06:41,032 --> 01:06:42,780
it really did learn a good density model.

1471
01:06:42,780 --> 01:06:45,240
And here's the density over the latent space,

1472
01:06:45,240 --> 01:06:47,660
it becomes kind of a unit Gaussian in two dimensions.

1473
01:06:47,660 --> 01:06:48,820
That looks like just--

1474
01:06:48,820 --> 01:06:51,400
like a little blip of bell-shaped probability.

1475
01:06:53,940 --> 01:06:57,620
OK, so VAE is really just three tricks--

1476
01:06:57,620 --> 01:07:00,580
approximate an infinite mixture with samples,

1477
01:07:00,580 --> 01:07:02,667
sample efficiently by not just sampling at random,

1478
01:07:02,667 --> 01:07:04,500
but with importance samples-- so approximate

1479
01:07:04,500 --> 01:07:07,300
with important samples, and predict

1480
01:07:07,300 --> 01:07:10,220
the optimal important samples to take the optimal importance

1481
01:07:10,220 --> 01:07:12,420
sampling distribution with another neural network,

1482
01:07:12,420 --> 01:07:14,128
and then jointly optimize the whole thing

1483
01:07:14,128 --> 01:07:17,380
and it looks like an autoencoder.

1484
01:07:17,380 --> 01:07:19,380
OK.

1485
01:07:19,380 --> 01:07:22,818
So in case some of that was a little bit too abstract,

1486
01:07:22,818 --> 01:07:24,860
I just wanted to give you the step by step, like,

1487
01:07:24,860 --> 01:07:27,027
what would you write in PyTorch to make this happen?

1488
01:07:27,027 --> 01:07:29,380
It's actually very simple.

1489
01:07:29,380 --> 01:07:31,940
So to optimize a VAE, I'll sample a data

1490
01:07:31,940 --> 01:07:34,660
point x from my training set--

1491
01:07:34,660 --> 01:07:36,040
or maybe a batch of data points.

1492
01:07:36,040 --> 01:07:39,220
I'll encode the data with a forward pass through my encoder.

1493
01:07:39,220 --> 01:07:41,280
For each data point that's been encoded,

1494
01:07:41,280 --> 01:07:45,320
I'll add some noise to my encoding.

1495
01:07:45,320 --> 01:07:47,780
That's my important sample.

1496
01:07:47,780 --> 01:07:50,952
Then I'll decode the data by passing it through the decoder.

1497
01:07:50,952 --> 01:07:52,660
It'll compute the losses, which will just

1498
01:07:52,660 --> 01:07:55,980
be the squared error and the KL term.

1499
01:07:55,980 --> 01:07:59,060
And then that will give me a scalar loss

1500
01:07:59,060 --> 01:08:01,420
value at the end of the day, and I'll just backprop

1501
01:08:01,420 --> 01:08:04,740
through that computation graph.

1502
01:08:04,740 --> 01:08:06,948
And typically for the VAE, even though I would really

1503
01:08:06,948 --> 01:08:08,573
want to take a lot of important samples

1504
01:08:08,573 --> 01:08:10,880
to get a better estimate, usually people just take one.

1505
01:08:10,880 --> 01:08:16,140
They do forward one sample, decode backward, and that

1506
01:08:16,140 --> 01:08:17,520
seems to be sufficient.

1507
01:08:17,520 --> 01:08:18,020
Yeah?

1508
01:08:18,020 --> 01:08:20,020
AUDIENCE: How do you backprop through sampling?

1509
01:08:20,020 --> 01:08:21,600
PHILLIP ISOLA: So that's why that reparametrize.

1510
01:08:21,600 --> 01:08:22,640
How do you backprop through sampling?

1511
01:08:22,640 --> 01:08:24,473
That's why that reparameterization trick was

1512
01:08:24,473 --> 01:08:26,687
important, because the stochastic element comes

1513
01:08:26,687 --> 01:08:28,979
in through the side of the graph and you don't actually

1514
01:08:28,979 --> 01:08:31,300
have to backprop through that sampling procedure.

1515
01:08:31,300 --> 01:08:31,899
Yeah.

1516
01:08:31,899 --> 01:08:34,316
You're just backdropping through the mean and the variance

1517
01:08:34,316 --> 01:08:36,420
of a distribution, and the actual noise

1518
01:08:36,420 --> 01:08:40,660
is coming through the side and doesn't affect the optimization.

1519
01:08:40,660 --> 01:08:42,180
A question in the back.

1520
01:08:42,180 --> 01:08:44,100
AUDIENCE: In practice, is it important to get

1521
01:08:44,100 --> 01:08:48,300
the variance inside the latent, like sigmas,

1522
01:08:48,300 --> 01:08:49,520
from the neural network?

1523
01:08:49,520 --> 01:08:53,029
Or does it also work to just have always the same one?

1524
01:08:53,029 --> 01:08:54,779
Because it would be natural to think like,

1525
01:08:54,779 --> 01:08:56,279
oh, we should just do an autoencoder

1526
01:08:56,279 --> 01:08:57,540
and you just noise it.

1527
01:08:57,540 --> 01:09:01,048
Some things will be smoother than the representation,

1528
01:09:01,048 --> 01:09:03,340
but maybe it doesn't matter to have different variances

1529
01:09:03,340 --> 01:09:04,160
for different--

1530
01:09:04,160 --> 01:09:04,993
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1531
01:09:04,993 --> 01:09:06,319
So great, yeah, good question.

1532
01:09:06,319 --> 01:09:08,600
The question is, for the encoder,

1533
01:09:08,600 --> 01:09:13,340
is it important that the noise you're adding actually

1534
01:09:13,340 --> 01:09:17,620
is dependent on the variance predicted by the neural net?

1535
01:09:17,620 --> 01:09:19,540
So you're kind of asking, for q Z

1536
01:09:19,540 --> 01:09:21,979
given x, which you're modeling, would it

1537
01:09:21,979 --> 01:09:25,700
suffice to just model the mean and the variance

1538
01:09:25,700 --> 01:09:27,779
to be 1 or some constant?

1539
01:09:27,779 --> 01:09:32,020
And I think, indeed, oftentimes you

1540
01:09:32,020 --> 01:09:33,760
can get away with those types of things.

1541
01:09:33,760 --> 01:09:35,540
Like, you can just take an autoencoder,

1542
01:09:35,540 --> 01:09:38,520
add a little noise to the latent space and do nothing else,

1543
01:09:38,520 --> 01:09:40,020
and it might be fine.

1544
01:09:40,020 --> 01:09:43,380
Or maybe you add this KL penalty to say, take an autoencoder,

1545
01:09:43,380 --> 01:09:45,500
penalize the means for being far from the origin,

1546
01:09:45,500 --> 01:09:49,420
and add a little noise, and it'll give you a VAE

1547
01:09:49,420 --> 01:09:51,260
and it might be fine.

1548
01:09:51,260 --> 01:09:54,002
But your mileage will vary, and you can always also

1549
01:09:54,002 --> 01:09:56,460
not model these things with Gaussians, but more complicated

1550
01:09:56,460 --> 01:09:57,460
distributions.

1551
01:09:57,460 --> 01:09:59,280
So there's a lot of variations on this,

1552
01:09:59,280 --> 01:10:03,017
but the simplest vanilla version might be good enough.

1553
01:10:03,017 --> 01:10:04,600
And I think it captures the full idea.

1554
01:10:04,600 --> 01:10:06,017
Like, the simplest vanilla version

1555
01:10:06,017 --> 01:10:08,500
is just smooth out the latent space

1556
01:10:08,500 --> 01:10:13,060
and make it dense by penalizing the encodings from being far

1557
01:10:13,060 --> 01:10:15,980
from the origin, and smooth it out by adding some noise

1558
01:10:15,980 --> 01:10:20,120
so that it has finite information capacity,

1559
01:10:20,120 --> 01:10:23,700
it can't overfit or have too complicated a decoding mapping.

1560
01:10:23,700 --> 01:10:24,200
OK.

1561
01:10:24,200 --> 01:10:26,900
Anyway, more to say about that.

1562
01:10:26,900 --> 01:10:27,700
Yeah, question?

1563
01:10:27,700 --> 01:10:30,340
AUDIENCE: Is there a reason why pushing the latent variable

1564
01:10:30,340 --> 01:10:34,387
distribution to 0 is better other than mathematics?

1565
01:10:34,387 --> 01:10:35,220
PHILLIP ISOLA: Yeah.

1566
01:10:35,220 --> 01:10:35,920
So, right.

1567
01:10:35,920 --> 01:10:38,700
So why is pushing the latent variable distribution to 0

1568
01:10:38,700 --> 01:10:39,200
better.

1569
01:10:39,200 --> 01:10:47,020
So it comes out as that is what KL divergence between a unit

1570
01:10:47,020 --> 01:10:49,360
Gaussian and another Gaussian will do.

1571
01:10:52,020 --> 01:10:53,540
But I think more intuitively, it's

1572
01:10:53,540 --> 01:10:55,790
just-- it's this kind of sphere-packing an idea

1573
01:10:55,790 --> 01:11:02,990
that I want it to be the case that every single point

1574
01:11:02,990 --> 01:11:05,170
in z-space, if I sample from that point,

1575
01:11:05,170 --> 01:11:09,470
it will lead to a valid image or a valid decoded data point x.

1576
01:11:09,470 --> 01:11:11,750
And if I don't pack things in, if I

1577
01:11:11,750 --> 01:11:15,030
don't squish all the means towards 0,

1578
01:11:15,030 --> 01:11:16,950
then there might be gaps.

1579
01:11:16,950 --> 01:11:22,213
So, like here, there might be these gaps.

1580
01:11:22,213 --> 01:11:24,630
At the end of the optimization, there's a little gap here.

1581
01:11:24,630 --> 01:11:27,047
That might-- I mean, this is only a finite set of samples,

1582
01:11:27,047 --> 01:11:29,750
but maybe there's no--

1583
01:11:29,750 --> 01:11:32,110
these data points here, like, they

1584
01:11:32,110 --> 01:11:34,470
can't be reconstructed at all.

1585
01:11:34,470 --> 01:11:36,950
Or equivalently on this direction,

1586
01:11:36,950 --> 01:11:40,842
if there's gaps here, if these means all went out

1587
01:11:40,842 --> 01:11:43,050
to infinity-- so it's like, oh, I have high capacity,

1588
01:11:43,050 --> 01:11:44,870
I can memorize everything, they're

1589
01:11:44,870 --> 01:11:47,245
going to go out to infinity, so they'll all be infinitely

1590
01:11:47,245 --> 01:11:49,750
far apart, and then I can more easily

1591
01:11:49,750 --> 01:11:52,650
decode where they came from-- there's no overlap at all,

1592
01:11:52,650 --> 01:11:54,830
it's like perfectly information-preserving,

1593
01:11:54,830 --> 01:11:57,470
it's invertible, well, that wouldn't be good because there'd

1594
01:11:57,470 --> 01:11:58,470
be all these gaps.

1595
01:11:58,470 --> 01:12:00,930
Where if I now take a sample from my z-space,

1596
01:12:00,930 --> 01:12:02,170
I might end up in a gap.

1597
01:12:02,170 --> 01:12:04,650
That's never-- so the decoder has never seen that sample

1598
01:12:04,650 --> 01:12:07,573
and it will not know what data point to reconstruct.

1599
01:12:07,573 --> 01:12:08,990
So you want there to be no gaps so

1600
01:12:08,990 --> 01:12:11,070
that any point you sample in that z-space

1601
01:12:11,070 --> 01:12:17,190
will map to a valid data point x according to your distribution

1602
01:12:17,190 --> 01:12:18,330
of training points x.

1603
01:12:18,330 --> 01:12:18,830
OK.

1604
01:12:18,830 --> 01:12:22,750
That's the intuition I have.

1605
01:12:22,750 --> 01:12:25,490
OK, so in the last five or 10 minutes,

1606
01:12:25,490 --> 01:12:29,190
I want to talk about what kinds of representations

1607
01:12:29,190 --> 01:12:33,030
are learned by a VAE and other generative models.

1608
01:12:33,030 --> 01:12:40,350
And so we're going to go back to this example here,

1609
01:12:40,350 --> 01:12:42,750
and we're going to ask, are the latent

1610
01:12:42,750 --> 01:12:46,710
variables z that the VAE learns actually

1611
01:12:46,710 --> 01:12:48,440
related to what I gave as an intuition?

1612
01:12:48,440 --> 01:12:50,190
I said I could write a Python program that

1613
01:12:50,190 --> 01:12:53,030
takes a normal distribution, maps it

1614
01:12:53,030 --> 01:12:54,870
to grass color; a Bernoulli distribution,

1615
01:12:54,870 --> 01:12:56,950
maps it to the turns of the river.

1616
01:12:56,950 --> 01:12:59,750
Well, is that what actually happens

1617
01:12:59,750 --> 01:13:04,070
when I train a model without hand-defining it?

1618
01:13:04,070 --> 01:13:08,150
OK, so I will take my data, I'll learn my encoder and my decoder.

1619
01:13:08,150 --> 01:13:11,830
My decoder can generate samples, and this is a good generative

1620
01:13:11,830 --> 01:13:13,270
model of my data.

1621
01:13:13,270 --> 01:13:15,090
But now let's look at the encoder.

1622
01:13:15,090 --> 01:13:17,150
Is the encoder separately useful?

1623
01:13:17,150 --> 01:13:20,910
Is a VAE a good representation learner?

1624
01:13:20,910 --> 01:13:23,450
OK, so we'll visualize it like this.

1625
01:13:23,450 --> 01:13:25,990
We're going to take our latent space

1626
01:13:25,990 --> 01:13:30,470
and we're going to move in axis line directions in the latent

1627
01:13:30,470 --> 01:13:34,830
space and see what images those map to.

1628
01:13:34,830 --> 01:13:38,910
OK, so if I move along one axis of latent space,

1629
01:13:38,910 --> 01:13:41,550
it ends up causing this kind of smooth variation

1630
01:13:41,550 --> 01:13:45,390
in the output space where the curvature of the river changes.

1631
01:13:45,390 --> 01:13:48,750
If I move along another dimension of latent space,

1632
01:13:48,750 --> 01:13:50,950
it ends up changing the color of the grass.

1633
01:13:50,950 --> 01:13:52,310
Oh, sorry.

1634
01:13:52,310 --> 01:13:54,230
OK.

1635
01:13:54,230 --> 01:14:00,670
So, indeed, my learned latent distribution

1636
01:14:00,670 --> 01:14:04,890
ends up having different dimensions in latent space

1637
01:14:04,890 --> 01:14:10,750
controlling different types of visual variation on this data.

1638
01:14:10,750 --> 01:14:12,870
And those types of visual variation

1639
01:14:12,870 --> 01:14:21,350
actually do relate to the underlying parameters that

1640
01:14:21,350 --> 01:14:23,370
define causally how the data was generated.

1641
01:14:23,370 --> 01:14:25,510
Like, there is a causal process, a Python program

1642
01:14:25,510 --> 01:14:29,950
I wrote that takes these attributes and turns them

1643
01:14:29,950 --> 01:14:32,310
into rivers and grass and so forth.

1644
01:14:32,310 --> 01:14:35,230
And one of those attributes is the grass color

1645
01:14:35,230 --> 01:14:36,370
is a normal distribution.

1646
01:14:36,370 --> 01:14:39,390
So there's one dimension of the latent space

1647
01:14:39,390 --> 01:14:42,770
that generated the data that should control grass color,

1648
01:14:42,770 --> 01:14:44,750
and the VAE actually recovers that.

1649
01:14:44,750 --> 01:14:47,830
So it identifies that true factor of--

1650
01:14:47,830 --> 01:14:50,070
that true causal factor in the data.

1651
01:14:50,070 --> 01:14:53,037
OK, but this is not always going to be the case.

1652
01:14:53,037 --> 01:14:55,370
The other factor of variation that I'm showing you here,

1653
01:14:55,370 --> 01:14:56,870
which is the curvature of the river,

1654
01:14:56,870 --> 01:15:00,470
actually does not correspond to any of the underlying

1655
01:15:00,470 --> 01:15:02,210
variables in my Python code.

1656
01:15:02,210 --> 01:15:04,630
Because the Python code took a vector of Bernoulli coin

1657
01:15:04,630 --> 01:15:07,430
flips to determine the shape of the river,

1658
01:15:07,430 --> 01:15:11,510
and there was no single variable, no Gaussian variable

1659
01:15:11,510 --> 01:15:14,930
that defined the overall shape of the river,

1660
01:15:14,930 --> 01:15:16,730
it was just a sequence of Bernoulli steps.

1661
01:15:16,730 --> 01:15:20,310
But somehow, the VAE learned this abstracted, intuitive

1662
01:15:20,310 --> 01:15:21,210
physics of the world.

1663
01:15:21,210 --> 01:15:23,252
It didn't actually learn the physics of the world

1664
01:15:23,252 --> 01:15:25,350
that I wrote in Python to generate this data,

1665
01:15:25,350 --> 01:15:27,763
it came up with this abstracted abstraction,

1666
01:15:27,763 --> 01:15:30,430
which is that there should be a factor of variation in this data

1667
01:15:30,430 --> 01:15:32,630
that controls the curvature of the river that's

1668
01:15:32,630 --> 01:15:34,150
a good explanation.

1669
01:15:34,150 --> 01:15:37,570
So a VAE, like other representation learners,

1670
01:15:37,570 --> 01:15:41,990
can learn different independent factors of variation

1671
01:15:41,990 --> 01:15:45,550
that affect the primary independent--

1672
01:15:45,550 --> 01:15:48,853
it can learn different latent variables, different dimensions

1673
01:15:48,853 --> 01:15:51,270
of latent space that control different independent factors

1674
01:15:51,270 --> 01:15:53,430
of variation-- we call that disentanglement,

1675
01:15:53,430 --> 01:15:55,430
that if I vary the river or curvature dimension,

1676
01:15:55,430 --> 01:15:57,680
it does not affect the color, and if I vary the color,

1677
01:15:57,680 --> 01:15:59,870
it does not affect the river curvature dimension.

1678
01:15:59,870 --> 01:16:02,430
This is a disentangled representation

1679
01:16:02,430 --> 01:16:03,910
where there's independent--

1680
01:16:03,910 --> 01:16:06,845
independence between the effects of those two factors,

1681
01:16:06,845 --> 01:16:08,470
but those two factors don't necessarily

1682
01:16:08,470 --> 01:16:12,070
map on to actually the causal mechanisms underlying

1683
01:16:12,070 --> 01:16:14,030
how this world was defined.

1684
01:16:14,030 --> 01:16:17,150
They might be more abstracted from it.

1685
01:16:17,150 --> 01:16:17,830
OK.

1686
01:16:17,830 --> 01:16:20,510
So VAEs are not the only models that

1687
01:16:20,510 --> 01:16:23,270
have this property of learning organized latent spaces that

1688
01:16:23,270 --> 01:16:26,270
are good maps or good representations of the world.

1689
01:16:26,270 --> 01:16:29,950
In fact, this is an example of a GAN, and it does--

1690
01:16:29,950 --> 01:16:31,810
has just the same types of properties.

1691
01:16:31,810 --> 01:16:38,550
So we covered GANs very briefly last lecture, and it's not--

1692
01:16:38,550 --> 01:16:42,690
GANs didn't have any encoder that maps from data to latents.

1693
01:16:42,690 --> 01:16:44,790
So VAEs do have an encoder, they can go from data

1694
01:16:44,790 --> 01:16:48,470
to the representation of the data in this disentangled space.

1695
01:16:48,470 --> 01:16:50,470
Again, doesn't have that, but nonetheless,

1696
01:16:50,470 --> 01:16:54,390
the latent variables, if you sample random latent variables

1697
01:16:54,390 --> 01:16:56,512
or sample along a grid of latent variables,

1698
01:16:56,512 --> 01:16:58,470
they do show that they actually model structure

1699
01:16:58,470 --> 01:17:00,630
in a similar way to the VAE.

1700
01:17:00,630 --> 01:17:04,510
And here's just an example from a model called BigGAN where

1701
01:17:04,510 --> 01:17:06,532
there's one direction in latent space

1702
01:17:06,532 --> 01:17:08,990
that controls the orientation of the bird and one direction

1703
01:17:08,990 --> 01:17:11,830
that controls the background color.

1704
01:17:11,830 --> 01:17:13,510
And you'll see this over and over again.

1705
01:17:13,510 --> 01:17:15,590
In all the types of generative models

1706
01:17:15,590 --> 01:17:21,190
that we have talked about, there are some kind

1707
01:17:21,190 --> 01:17:24,070
of latent variables usually, like in diffusion models, maybe

1708
01:17:24,070 --> 01:17:26,390
the noise.

1709
01:17:26,390 --> 01:17:28,350
Or there are other intermediate variables

1710
01:17:28,350 --> 01:17:31,030
like the activations of the network as it processes--

1711
01:17:31,030 --> 01:17:33,650
as a decoder processes the data.

1712
01:17:33,650 --> 01:17:35,150
The activations on some layer, I can

1713
01:17:35,150 --> 01:17:37,910
look at directions in that activation space.

1714
01:17:37,910 --> 01:17:42,350
And these tend to learn structured representations

1715
01:17:42,350 --> 01:17:42,910
of the data.

1716
01:17:42,910 --> 01:17:47,200
That is the mechanism by which the model can fit to the world

1717
01:17:47,200 --> 01:17:47,820
effectively.

1718
01:17:47,820 --> 01:17:51,432
It has to learn some kind of factors that can parsimoniously

1719
01:17:51,432 --> 01:17:52,140
explain the data.

1720
01:17:52,140 --> 01:17:54,307
If it only has so much capacity, so many dimensions,

1721
01:17:54,307 --> 01:17:56,993
this is a kind of somehow pops out

1722
01:17:56,993 --> 01:17:59,160
that this is a good representation of the world that

1723
01:17:59,160 --> 01:18:03,320
allows you to generate photos that maximize likelihood.

1724
01:18:03,320 --> 01:18:04,160
OK.

1725
01:18:04,160 --> 01:18:07,920
So generative modeling forces you to explain the world.

1726
01:18:07,920 --> 01:18:13,400
Explaining the world is done well by actually capturing

1727
01:18:13,400 --> 01:18:16,040
independent components and things that we

1728
01:18:16,040 --> 01:18:18,100
have physical names for, like background color,

1729
01:18:18,100 --> 01:18:19,820
but sometimes things are more abstracted,

1730
01:18:19,820 --> 01:18:23,060
like low-frequency curvature of the river.

1731
01:18:27,160 --> 01:18:31,760
Yeah, this one maybe just a kind of fun visualization to end on.

1732
01:18:31,760 --> 01:18:35,280
This is, again, that same map.

1733
01:18:35,280 --> 01:18:37,200
And here, I'm coloring the input points

1734
01:18:37,200 --> 01:18:40,080
according to their coordinates within this space.

1735
01:18:40,080 --> 01:18:42,240
And here's what happens as I train it.

1736
01:18:42,240 --> 01:18:47,160
So what you can see is that this thing

1737
01:18:47,160 --> 01:18:52,440
gets-- the points in this space get remapped to the sphere,

1738
01:18:52,440 --> 01:18:54,500
but not like in a disorganized way.

1739
01:18:54,500 --> 01:18:56,420
It's smooth, it's a smooth map.

1740
01:18:56,420 --> 01:18:59,295
And that's kind of why--

1741
01:18:59,295 --> 01:19:00,920
it's meant to give an intuition for how

1742
01:19:00,920 --> 01:19:04,120
these dimensions of the latent space

1743
01:19:04,120 --> 01:19:07,260
might map to smooth properties of the input space,

1744
01:19:07,260 --> 01:19:08,880
because on this simple example, all

1745
01:19:08,880 --> 01:19:10,840
it's really doing is squishing the thing down.

1746
01:19:10,840 --> 01:19:12,340
But there's also an important point,

1747
01:19:12,340 --> 01:19:16,300
which is that it didn't quite fill this gap here.

1748
01:19:16,300 --> 01:19:18,760
And if it really was going to squish it into a sphere,

1749
01:19:18,760 --> 01:19:21,040
a Gaussian, then it should fill that.

1750
01:19:21,040 --> 01:19:23,687
And you'll see this when you're training generative models,

1751
01:19:23,687 --> 01:19:26,020
that in the latent space, things will be smooth and nice

1752
01:19:26,020 --> 01:19:27,720
and there will be a seam because it's

1753
01:19:27,720 --> 01:19:30,160
like you're mapping this complicated topology

1754
01:19:30,160 --> 01:19:33,400
onto a sphere, and you're going to have cuts and seams,

1755
01:19:33,400 --> 01:19:34,580
and you see it right here.

1756
01:19:34,580 --> 01:19:36,520
So if you're navigating through VAE latent

1757
01:19:36,520 --> 01:19:39,400
space or diffusion model activation space

1758
01:19:39,400 --> 01:19:41,342
on some layer of the network, you'll

1759
01:19:41,342 --> 01:19:42,800
come across these weird jumps where

1760
01:19:42,800 --> 01:19:44,300
it's smooth moves, nice variation,

1761
01:19:44,300 --> 01:19:46,640
and then suddenly it switches to a different category,

1762
01:19:46,640 --> 01:19:49,260
and that's what's going on here.

1763
01:19:49,260 --> 01:19:49,760
OK.

1764
01:19:49,760 --> 01:19:51,240
Also, these tendrils.

1765
01:19:51,240 --> 01:19:53,980
Like, you can't-- this is all smooth transformations.

1766
01:19:53,980 --> 01:19:54,940
How do you cut?

1767
01:19:54,940 --> 01:19:59,640
You can't really cut, so you get this tendril of probability.

1768
01:19:59,640 --> 01:20:01,450
OK, so these are the models that we've

1769
01:20:01,450 --> 01:20:02,700
seen in the last two lectures.

1770
01:20:02,700 --> 01:20:03,720
We covered a lot.

1771
01:20:03,720 --> 01:20:07,040
So we talked about a bunch.

1772
01:20:07,040 --> 01:20:09,820
I'd say I think the most popular right now is diffusion models.

1773
01:20:09,820 --> 01:20:11,760
The most complete, like full picture,

1774
01:20:11,760 --> 01:20:13,640
that encompasses everything is VAEs.

1775
01:20:13,640 --> 01:20:15,683
The most cute and fun is GANs.

1776
01:20:15,683 --> 01:20:17,600
The ones that are popular in language modeling

1777
01:20:17,600 --> 01:20:18,675
is autoregressive.

1778
01:20:18,675 --> 01:20:20,300
And they all have different trade-offs.

1779
01:20:20,300 --> 01:20:22,508
Some of them have latent variables that are explicit,

1780
01:20:22,508 --> 01:20:23,160
some don't.

1781
01:20:23,160 --> 01:20:25,540
Some give you a density or energy function, some don't.

1782
01:20:25,540 --> 01:20:27,873
And some give you a way of sampling efficiently and some

1783
01:20:27,873 --> 01:20:28,680
don't.

1784
01:20:28,680 --> 01:20:31,480
So that's for your own reference to review.

1785
01:20:31,480 --> 01:20:33,590
OK, thank you.
